This observation motivates the use of Hawkes processes for modeling price dynamics, as they capture the self-exciting and clustering behaviors characteristic of high-frequency financial data, which are properties not well-modeled by Brownian motion or simpler point processes like Poisson processes. By allowing the intensity to depend on its own path, Hawkes processes naturally account for the autocorrelation present in mid-price returns and order arrivals. Another reason to favor Hawkes processes over, e.g., time inhomogeneous Poisson processes, is that time changed arrival times of market orders cannot be adequately modeled using exponential distributions~\cite{DG22}. Furthermore, we observe that the integrated volatility of the mid-price process varies over the trading horizon, increasing as gate closure nears (see Figure~\ref{fig:SignaturePlot3d1}). Again, this phenomenon reflects the heightened trading intensity and market activity driven by increasingly accurate renewable energy forecasts as delivery approaches. As traders receive more reliable information closer to delivery time, they adjust their positions accordingly, resulting in increased volatility.

