On the Buy side, the median excitation parameter, $\alpha$, ranges from $360$ to $450$ $(1/h)$. This indicates that following the arrival of a Buy market order, the conditional intensity increases by approximately $360$ to $450$ arrivals per hour. Similar values are observed on the Sell side, with minor variations across trading products. The decay rate, $\beta$, of the exponential kernel corresponds to a half-life of $\frac{\ln(2)}{\beta}$, approximately $1.5$ to $2$ seconds, suggesting that the self-excitation effect diminishes rapidly.

The median branching ratio, defined as $\frac{\alpha}{\beta}$ in the univariate case, represents the average number of arrivals triggered by the excitation from a single event. For the spline approximation, this ratio is approximately $0.30$ to $0.32$ for most calibrated products on the Buy side, satisfying the stationarity condition $\frac{\alpha}{\beta} < 1$ required for univariate exponential Hawkes processes \cite{Bacry2013}. This implies that the arrival of a Buy market order triggers, on average, an additional $0.30$ to $0.32$ Buy market orders.

Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS) tests reveal no significant differences at the $5\%$ level between the Buy and Sell sides in the distributions of excitation parameters and branching ratios. Additionally, no distinct patterns are observed in the calibrated parameters of the exponential excitation kernel across different delivery hours. However, the calibrated baseline intensity using the spline approximation exhibits noticeable variations across products (see Figure~\ref{fig: CalibratedSpline}), effectively capturing the observed increase in market activity for mid-day traded products, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:VarIntensity1}.

