\subparagraph{\bf 1.7.}  One might think of $C_n(G)$ as follows.  Suppose
$n$ (labelled) tokens sit on distinct vertices of $G$, and each 
token may move (along an edge) from
its vertex to an adjacent unoccupied vertex.  A collection of moves can be
executed simultaneously only if they can be done in any order.  With these
rules, the set of possible configurations of the tokens is $C_n(G)$.

