We have shown that rheoSCAT can extract detailed, spatially resolved information about the material properties and activity of cells, including overall activity levels, diffusion and the ratio of storage and loss moduli as a function of frequency ($G'(f)/G''(f)$). An outstanding question is whether it is possible to achieve an absolute calibration the complex shear modulus. Typically, such calibration requires detailed knowledge of the size and shape of the probe particles~\cite{brau2007passive}. It is conceivable that the ensemble of cytoplasmic proteins that contribute to the rheoSCAT signal is sufficiently uniform, within a given cell type, to permit such a calibration. Alternatively, in two-particle microrheology correlated particle motions allow calibration without detailed knowledge of the probe particles~\cite{levine2000one}. Similar techniques may be applicable to ensembles of correlated particles such as those in rheoSCAT. These remain open questions for future investigation.


