Our precision matrix estimator has three broad areas of interest. First, it can be used to construct debiased estimators for parameters in high-dimensional regression models. Debiased estimators are essential for constructing valid confidence intervals in high-dimensional settings (see \cite{canerkock2018}, \cite{van2014}). A key challenge in this context is obtaining an estimate of the inverse of the Gram matrix, as the sample second moment matrix is typically singular when the number of variables exceeds the sample size. Our estimator provides a viable alternative for estimating this inverse, and can be readily adapted for use in such models. Second, in network analysis, under  normally distributed random outcome variables, conditional dependence between two random variables is represented as a nonzero term in the precision matrix of all outcome variables. Our method avoids imposing ``artificial'' conditional independence assumptions—i.e., forcing zero entries in the precision matrix that correspond to the absence of links between variables. Instead, it preserves all connections in the network, allowing some to be minor and others to be dominant. A direct application of such setting is the study of tax interactions between US states in the study of \cite{prs2024}. They impose sparsity of their social interaction matrix  i.e., zero interactions between states  (see equations (8) and (10) of their paper). Third, in financial econometrics, our estimator can be used where precision matrices of asset returns play a central role in tasks such as optimal portfolio allocation, portfolio variance estimation, and Sharpe Ratio calculation. 
A detailed literature review and discussion of these three broad applications is provided in Section \ref{examples}.
