\subsection{ Dimension Reduction}

	Why do we use a hidden factor setup?  First of all, we show that precision matrix estimation is related to linear regression and that can be related to hidden factors (see Online Appendix B). Each economic or financial variable can be related to latent factors and this can be thought as confounding variables as in causality related literature such as in \cite{kv2023}, \cite{ap2009}, \cite{ir2015}. 
	 Also the true variables of interest may be measured with errors, and latent factors can represent the "true" variables rather than the reported ones, especially in emerging markets. Variable such as culture, sentiment, market can be extremely valuable in certain macro, financial data sets. These hidden factors in a linear model can be used to explain international stock returns such as in Singapore, Malaysia, USA as seen in \cite{bn2004}.

Another key aspect of using hidden factors is their effectiveness as a tool for dimension reduction in high-dimensional econometrics. This stems from the fact that the number of observed outcomes typically exceeds the number of underlying hidden factors. In our paper, we allow {\it both} the number of outcomes and the number of hidden factors to grow with the sample size. The role and significance of latent factors and the estimation of their key components, factors and loadings, have been largely extended and further linked to asset pricing in \cite{lp2020}, causal inference and estimation of counterfactuals or treatment effects  in \cite{ABDQ19,ABDIK21,YanWain24,KallusNIPS}, recommenders systems in \cite{Agarwal2021}, macroeconomic forecasting in \cite{StockW02,StockW02b,StockW02c}, matrix completion and missing panel data in \cite{XPlatfac10,CBN21,BaiNg21b,DPX23,Pelgermissingness}. \cite{CF2024} discuss how latent factors can model important features of network data such as reciprocity, degree heterogeneity, and homophily. 
	They apply these methods to the estimation of gravity equations of trade between countries, showing how unobserved latent factors significantly influence observed trade volumes, even after controlling  for traditional gravity variables like distance.
	Excellent theoretical and practical algorithmic results obtained in all these works usually serve as a strong indication that there is indeed a very strong rationale for utilization of latent factors. Somewhat paradoxically, while we will rely on similar factor modeling concepts and underlying intuitions that they bring along, we will be able to design new and novel precision matrix estimators without actually focusing on directly estimating factors themselves.



\subsection{A Brief Discussion of Sparsity in Economics}

    Our proposed class of estimators is broadly applicable across a range of settings, including those where the underlying data do not exhibit sparsity. For instance, \cite{glp2021} analyze six widely used datasets in macroeconomics, microeconomics, and finance, and find that dense models consistently outperform sparse ones in terms of out-of-sample predictive accuracy. These applications include: (i) forecasting U.S. economic activity, (ii) identifying determinants of economic growth across countries, (iii) explaining the decline in crime rates across U.S. states, (iv) analyzing judicial rulings on eminent domain across U.S. circuits, (v) predicting the U.S. equity premium, and (vi) accounting for the cross-sectional variation in U.S. stock returns.

    In asset pricing, the assumption of sparsity for explaining or predicting economic variables is particularly strong and has been critically examined (see p.33 and Section 3.7 of \cite{n2021}). In that study, sparsity is imposed between the outcome variable and the predictors, effectively forcing some coefficients to be zero—even if those predictors have a non-negligible but small relationship with the outcome. This can be problematic in settings where many variables contribute modestly to predictive performance. Techniques like Lasso may shrink all small coefficients to zero, resulting in an artificially sparse model and a significant loss of predictive power.\footnote{Figure 3.2 of \cite{n2021} illustrates how sparsity-based methods can underperform due to this limitation.} 



	\subsection{Literature Survey}\label{litsurv}




	Several studies in the statistics literature have studied precision matrix estimation. An excellent survey is by \cite{crz2016}. In Section 3 of their paper, they provide 
two major approaches to precision matrix estimation. The first approach is neighborhood-based and closely related to nodewise regression, where a lasso regression is performed for each variable against all others. Alternatives to lasso, such as the Dantzig selector, can also be used within this framework (see \cite{cl2011}, \cite{caner2019}). The second major approach is based on penalized likelihood estimation. As shown by \cite{blp2020}, lasso-type penalties in this context can induce shrinkage bias and lead to overly sparse precision matrix estimates, with many zero entries in each row. In a penalized likelihood based setting, they reformulate the problem as a mixed-integer optimization problem, which is solvable using convex optimization techniques. This approach produces better results than lasso based approaches. Sparsity of precision matrix is used in these two main approaches.

A distinct approach, commonly used in finance and statistics, derives the precision matrix by inverting the covariance matrix using the Sherman–Morrison–Woodbury (SMW) formula. In this framework, sparsity is assumed in the covariance matrix of the idiosyncratic errors, while asset returns are modeled through a factor structure (see \cite{fan2013}, \cite{caner2022}).

Our approach is different from the
three approaches discussed above, and provides a new way of solving the same problem. Compared to the first
two, we are not assuming exact sparsity in the precision matrix of outcomes. Compared to the last one, we are not using SMW formula to
invert the covariance matrix. This allows us to bypass imposing a structure
on the outcomes utilized in the covariance inversion. Instead we use a new
precision matrix formula without sparsity and rely on hidden factors to generate each
outcome. This effectively ensures that we do not benefit from the SMW matrix inversion formula. To be clear, we use model-free dense estimation, but consistency in high dimensions is achieved through hidden factor based dimension reduction without a need to estimate factors or identify them.

