Note that nodewise regression based estimator of \cite{caner2019} 
uses sparsity of precision matrix of outcomes. Then residual nodewise regression estimator of  \cite{caner2022} crucially depends on observed factors, and imposes sparse precision matrix of errors. Technique used in those papers cannot extend to hidden factors  since observed factor structure in \cite{caner2022} uses OLS estimation which is not applicable to more complicated estimation problems such as hidden factor structure. Note that \cite{caner2019} has no factor structure and uses lasso based regression to estimate the precision matrix of outcomes.

We also tie our results to signal-to-noise ratio aspect which is not available in \cite{caner2019}, \cite{caner2022} papers. We show that to get consistency of our estimators, signal-to-noise ratio should be larger than number of factors. Our method can be seen as a complementary approach to nodewise regression of \cite{mein2006}. Nodewise regression uses an $l_1$ norm based penalization to form the rows of the precision matrix. Doing so, the underlying assumption  is the imposition of sparsity in the precision matrix which may be interpreted as a strong assumption. 
