	In our setup we have $p$ outcome variables, and they are explained by $K$ common hidden factors to ensure dimension reduction, with $K <<p$. This will help in asymptotics and achieving consistency, however our estimators will be model-free and will not use hidden factors estimators. Our estimators will only use outcome variables and this will be clear in the next section. Specifically, our estimators are defined in (\ref{3.1})(\ref{14a}).
	Let $Y$ represent the $n \times p$ matrix of outcomes, each row representing the $t$ th time period, $t=1,\cdots,n$, and $j=1,\cdots,p$ representing the outcomes. $F$ represents $n \times K$ matrix of hidden factors, where each row, $t=1,\cdots, n$ represents a time period, and columns represent the hidden factors, $k=1,\cdots, K$. $A$ represents $K \times p$	matrix of factor loadings, and $U: n \times p$ as the error matrix. 
	 We have the following compact form of the linear hidden factor structure for dimension reduction (which will be decomposed via rows of Y into two equations immediately below in (\ref{1})-(\ref{2})).
