	%We believe our simulation exercise will provide answers.  


	 \item  Selection of the principal components is by the elbow method which depends on an infeasible lower bound. To have a feasible estimation, we follow (56) of \cite{bbsmw21} or (2.7) of \cite{bw19}. Assuming additionally in  Corollary \ref{cl1}  that  $tr (\Sigma_{U,-j} ) \ge c'' \| \Sigma_{U,-j} \|_2 (n \wedge p)$, with $c''>0$ a positive constant, for each $j=1,\cdots,p$,  Proposition 8 of \cite{bbsmw21} can be extended with a simple union bound and the feasible estimation of the unknown number of factors results do not change  Corollary \ref{cl1}.
	In that respect,  we set, for $j=1,\cdots,p$
	$ \tilde{s}_j:= \sum_{k=1}^{\bar{K}} 1_{ \{ \hat{\sigma}_k^2 (Y_{-j} ) \ge \mu_n \hat{v}_{k,j}^2
	\} },$
	where $\mu_n:= \frac{1}{4} (n+(p-1))$, and $\hat{\sigma}_k^2 (Y_{-j})$ is the $k$ th largest singular value (squared)  of $Y_{-j}: n \times p-1$ matrix, and 
	$\hat{v}_{k,j}^2:=  \frac{ \| Y_{-j} - Y_{-j,(k)} \|_F^2}{n (p-1) - \mu_n k},$
	with $Y_{-j, (k)}$ representing  the best $k$ rank approximation of $Y_{-j}$, (Singular value decomposition into largest  k singular values)$\|.\|_F$ is the Frobenius norm, and 
	$ \bar{K}:= \left \lfloor \frac{\kappa}{1+\kappa} \frac{n(p-1)} {\mu_n} \right \rfloor \wedge n \wedge p,$
	with $\left \lfloor x \right \rfloor$ represents the floor  function, which is the greatest  integer strictly less than real number x, $\kappa>1$ is a positive constant number.

   % \item Our results are different then risk analysis of single outcome  by adaptive PCR in Corollaries 5-6 of \cite{bbsmw21}.
\end{itemize}

\end{remark}

