\subsection{Uses of Precision Matrix Estimate} \label{examples}


	Here in this section we  discuss the usage of our precision matrix estimator in three different cases of interest in economics and finance. In addition to the examples that we will discuss below, we should also add that many of our results are of independent statistical interest as well. We single out two particular features of our statistical analyses, (i) lack of exact sparsity assumption between outcomes with model-free dense estimators and (ii) an explicit connection of the estimation error to signal-to-noise ratio, as results that significantly advance the current state of the art in the relevant statistics precision matrix estimation related literature.



	In financial econometrics and finance, one of the key issues is large portfolio formation. Define the number of  assets 
    of a large portfolio as $p$, and the sample size as $n$. 
Two precision matrix estimating approaches distinguished themselves in recent literature: (i) the first one that relies on the estimation and inversion of the covariance matrix (see, e.g., \cite{fan2013} and \cite{lw2017} where  covariance matrix is obtained via hidden factor model and shrinkage estimation, respectively);
    and (ii) the second one that directly estimates the precision matrix and relies on the so-called nodewise regression approach (see, e.g., \cite{caner2019} and \cite{caner2022} for  nodewise regression with observed factors). We introduce a new estimator that can be used to consistently estimate the Sharpe Ratio even when $p>n$ (Sharpe Ratio is one of the key practical financial metrics as it can measure return-risk ratio for a given portfolio).

   % High dimensional portfolio formation when $p>n$ is done using different precision matrix estimator of asset returns. Literature used factor model based covariance matrix of asset returns and then inverted it like in \cite{fan2013}, or shrinkage based estimation of covariance matrix of asset returns and inverting this by \cite{lw2017}, and nodewise based regression as in \cite{caner2019} or nodewise regression combined with observed factors as in \cite{caner2022}. One of the key metrics in practice in finance is Sharpe Ratio since it can measure return-risk ratio for a given portfolio. Our estimator can be used to estimate that ratio consistently even with $p>n$, and can perform well. 
	%We use that as a running example in our paper, and prove theorems about Sharpe Ratio in high dimensions. We also show that with one of our estimators, we can observe a double-ascent phenomenon in Sharpe Ratio and this is a new result, and theoretical and empirical evidence will support this new finding. Details  are in Section \ref{examples}.

	The second possibility is 
