\begin{center}	
{\bf \large Simulations}
\end{center}





By (2) and (3) and Assumption 4, for each $t=1,...,n$, we have:
\begin{equation}
    y_{t}= A  f_t + u_t, \label{eq:1}
\end{equation}
where $y_t = (y_{jt},Y_{-jt}')': p \times 1$ is the vector of excess return, $ A =(a_j,A'_{-j}): p\times K$ is the matrix of factor loadings, and $u_t=(u_{jt},U_{-jt})': p\times 1$ vector of errors. Let  hidden-factors  be normally distributed and $f_t \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_f, \Sigma_f)$, where $\mu_f$ is the mean vector of the hidden factor, $K\times 1$ vector with each element equal to 0.5, and $\Sigma_f$ is the hidden-factor covariance matrix. Following \cite{bbsmw21}, we set the diagonal elements of $\Sigma_f$  as the $K-$ length sequence from 2.5 to 3 with equal increments. For example, if $K=3$, then $\Sigma_f = diag(2.5,2.75,3)$. The the off diagonal, $i,j$ th elements of the factor covariance matrix are defined as:
  \begin{align}
     [\Sigma_f]_{i,j} = (-1)^{i+j}  (0.3)^{|i-j|} \min( [\Sigma_{f}]_{i,i}, [\Sigma_{f}]_{j,j} ).
\end{align}
Each element of $A$ is drawn independently from  the normal distribution $\mathcal{N} (0.5,1/\sqrt{K})$. The idiosyncratic error, $u_t$, is drawn from the normal distribution, $\mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_u)$. 

In our design, we define $\Sigma_u:p\times p$ as a diagonal matrix with diagonal elements sampled from uniform distribution with $\text{Unif}(1,3) \times \log(p)$. With that we capture noisy setups in complex models and can understand the effect of noise on adaptive PCR and RRE with $p-1>n$ specifically.
%In the second experiment, following the approach in \cite{bbsmw21}, $\Sigma_u$ is set as a diagonal matrix with diagonal elements sampled from $\text{Unif}(1,3)$. 
The results  for $K=3, 5$ (true number of  hidden factors) are  presented in Tables \ref{tab:K=3}-\ref{tab:K=5}, respectively. 
We also have the same design  with true number of latent factors as $K=20$, the results were very similar to $K=5$ hence not reported. This $K=20$ Table can be obtained from authors on demand.%while the results from the second experiment results are provided in the appendix, Table \ref{tab:K=3-Bing}-\ref{tab:K=20-Bing}. 
However note that we still use $K=20$ in other tables to see the effects of fitting larger number of factors when the true DGP has few factors.
In addition, to further demonstrate how our methods behave as the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) changes, we rewrite (\ref{eq:1}) in compact form: 
\begin{equation}
    Y = F A + U \label{eq:2}
\end{equation}

Similar to \cite{bbsmw21}, we multiply each element of $A$ by a scalar $\alpha$ chosen from the range ${0.1,...,1.5}$. Fixing $K=20,n=400$, we experiment in  high- dimensional case, with  $p=450$. 
The low dimensional case is almost identical to high dimensional case, so we report only high dimensional case. Then for each $\alpha$, we calculate the SNR and plot the precision matrix estimation error  for any of the utilized  models. The results are presented in Figure 1.

We aim to address several questions through simulations. First, as described in the theorems, we investigate whether the estimation errors from our methods decrease when $\gamma = p/n$  is fixed and both $p$ and $n$ jointly increase \footnote{$\gamma=p/n=0.5$ in low-dimensional set up  and $\gamma = p/n=1.5$ in high-dimensional set up }. Second, we compare the performance of our new methods with existing methods. Third, we examine the impact of increasing the number of hidden factors, and also  see whether there is a symptom of double descent in RRE estimator. Lastly, we study the impact of the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) on estimation when the number of hidden factors $K$ is fixed. 

\begin{center}
{\bf Metrics}
\end{center}

We consider metric of estimation error for the precision matrix as stated in Theorem 1. The model comparisons include several approaches from the literature. We consider the nonlinear shrinkage method of  \cite{lw2017}(NL-LW), POET method from \cite{fan2013}, and the nodewise (NW) method from  \cite{caner2019} We also tried single factor-nonlinear shrinkage method of \cite{lw2017} but the results were worse than   its no factor version of NL-LW so we decided to drop that. 
Our new method introduces several variations: RRE, PCR-3F, PCR-5F, PCR-20F and PCR-Adaptive. These denote the use of a RRE estimator, Principal component with fixed 3, 5, or 20 hidden factors, and an adaptive method to select the number of hidden factors, respectively. We do not include in the simulations, \cite{caner2022} residual nodewise since it only uses observed factors. We also have done some very limited simulations with 3 factors with residual nodewise via the design here, treating unknown factors as known,  and the results are similar to nodewise regression, hence omitted.


\begin{center}
{\bf Results}
\end{center}


Table \ref{tab:K=3}- \ref{tab:K=5} shows the simulation results. The values in each cell show the average absolute estimation error for estimating the precision matrix. Each two-column block in the tables shows the result for a different sample size. The first column present results for $p=n/2$ (low-dimensional case), while the second corresponds to  $p=3n/2$ (high-dimensional case). We ran 100 iterations in each simulation set up. 

First, the tables show that our method achieves consistency as stated in Corollary 4 for adaptive PCR. Analyzing Table \ref{tab:K=3} with $K=3$, we observe that for $p=n/2$, with $n=100$, our Adaptive-PCR has an estimation error at 0.0583. This error declines to 0.0251  when $p=n/2$ and $n=400$. This indicates that as both $n$ and $p$ increase jointly, the estimation error decreases. Note that our RRE estimator in low dimensions (OLS) is consistent only when $p/n\to0$ as shown in Corollary 5. However, it sill does well with $p=n/2$ in our Tables \ref{tab:K=3}- \ref{tab:K=5}. To give an example, from Table \ref{tab:K=3}, the error is 0.4583 at $n=100$, and declines to 0.3369 at $n=400$. We also experimented with $p/n \to 0$ in our setup. As expected RRE error terms decline to zero fast, and these results are available from authors on demand.


%To give another example, but from a high dimensional case, from Table \ref{tab:K=20} with $K=20$, the estimation error of our Adaptive-PCR is 0.0674 and RRE is 0.1554 , with $p=3n/2,n=100$, then these errors decline to 0.0359 and 0.1313 with $p=3n/2, n=400$.  Note that RRE in high dimensions do not need $p/n\to0$ as this is clear from Corollary 6.


Next, we consider which method achieves the smallest estimation errors. Our Adaptive-PCR dominates all other techniques except fixed PCR-K. In additional, our Adaptive-PCR effectively identifies the correct number of hidden factors in almost all of the scenarios. For instance, at $p=3n/2,n=400$ number of factor $K =5 $ in Table \ref{tab:K=5}, the estimation errors  of both PCR-5F and Adaptive-PCR are the same at 0.0302, which also is the smallest estimation error among all methods. 



Next, the number of hidden factors used in our PCR methods---whether  underfitting or overfitting--- impacts the estimation error. When the correct number of hidden factors is \( K = 3 \), with \( n = 400 \) and \( p = 3n/2 \), the estimation error of PCR-3F is \( 0.0302 \) in Table \ref{tab:K=3}. However, overfitting the model by using \( K = 5,\, \text{or } 20 \) hidden factors increases the estimation error; for example, using PCR-20, the estimation error increases to \( 0.0401 \). Underfitting, becomes problematic with $n=400$ in Table \ref{tab:K=5}. In high dimensional scenario, PCR-5F has estimation error of 0.0302, whereas PCR-3F has error of 0.0524. As a side note to all of this, in RRE estimator, we see that estimation error for the low dimensional case is always higher than the double of high dimensional error scenario. This can be a symptom of double descent in RRE. To give an example, at Table \ref{tab:K=5} with $n=400$, the estimation error for RRE in low dimensional case is 0.3360, whereas in high dimensional case, RRE has error of 0.1417.








