\subsection{Cycles}\label{subsec:cycles}
\begin{example}[The cycle $C_d$] \label{ex:cycle}
Let $d \ge 3$. We will show for the $d$-cycle $C_d$ that we have
\begin{equation} \label{eq:nucycle}
\nu(C_d) = 2-2\cos(\pi/d). 
\end{equation}
We enumerate the vertices of $C_d$ by $x_0,x_1,\dots,x_{n-1}$ and impose the non-triviality of $\sigma$ only in the edge $\{x_{n-2},x_{n-1}\}$, that is 
$$ \sigma_t(x_{n-2},x_{n-1}) = e^{it}. $$
The Laplacian $\Delta^{\sigma_t}$ is then  represented by the following matrix:
$$ \Delta^{\sigma_t} \cong \begin{pmatrix} \begin{array}{ccccccc|c}
2 & -1 & 0 & \cdots & \cdots & 0 & 0 & -1 \\ 
-1 & 2 & -1 & & & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & -1 & 2 & \ddots & & 0 & 0& 0 \\
\vdots & & \ddots & \ddots & \ddots & & & \vdots \\
\vdots & & & \ddots & \ddots & \ddots & & \vdots \\
0 & 0 & 0 & & \ddots & 2 & -1 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 0 & & & -1 & 2 & -e^{it} \\
\hline
-1 & 0 & 0 & \cdots & \cdots & 0 & -e^{-it} & 2
\end{array} \end{pmatrix}, $$
and the eigenfunctions $f_j: C_d \to \C$ are illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:Cdeigfunc} with $\xi_d = e^{2 \pi i/d}$. Note that, in the case $t=0$, the red factors disappear and we end up with the eigenfunctions of the standard Laplacian on $C_d$. Moreover, we check for any $j_1\neq j_2$ that
\begin{align}\label{eq:orthogonality}
%\overline{f^{\top}_{j_1}}f_{j_2}
\langle f_{j_1},f_{j_2} \rangle
&=\sum_{\ell=0}^{d-2}\left(e^{\frac{it}{d}}\xi_{d}^{j_1}\right)^{\ell}\overline{\left(e^{\frac{it}{d}}\xi^{j_2}_{d}\right)^{\ell}}+\left(e^{\frac{it}{d}}\xi_{d}^{j_1}\right)^{-1}\overline{\left(e^{\frac{it}{d}}\xi_d^{j_2}\right)^{-1}}\notag\\
&=\sum_{\ell=-1}^{d-2}\xi_{d}^{(j_1-j_2)\ell}=\xi_{d}^{-(j_1-j_2)}\sum_{\ell=0}^{d-1}\xi_{d}^{(j_1-j_2)\ell}=0.
\end{align}
It is easy to check that the corresponding eigenvalue to $f_j$ is
\begin{equation} \label{eq:mujt} 
\mu_j(t) = 4 \sin^2\left( \frac{t+2\pi j}{2d}\right) = 2 - 2\cos\left(\frac{t+2\pi j}{d}\right) = 2 - \left( \xi_d^j e^{i \frac{t}{d}} + \xi_d^{-j} e^{-i \frac{t}{d}} \right). 
\end{equation}
Note that we have for $t \in [0,\pi]$,
$$ \cos\left(\frac{t}{d}\right) \ge \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{d}\right) \ge \cos\left( \frac{t+2j\pi}{d} \right), $$
since $\frac{t+2\pi j}{d} \in [\frac{2\pi}{d},2\pi-\frac{\pi}{d}]$ for $j=1,2,\dots,d-1$. This implies for $t \in [0,\pi]$ that
$$ \mu_0(t) \le \mu_j(t) \quad \text{for all $j=1,2,\dots,d-1$}, $$
with equality if and only if $t=\pi$ and $j=d-1$.
Moreover, we have for $t \in [0,\pi]$,
$$ \lambda_1(\Delta^{\sigma_t}) = \mu_0(t) = 2-2\cos(t/d).  $$
Since the eigenvalue graphs are symmetric with respect to $\pi$, we conclude that
$$
\nu(C_d) = \mu_0(\pi) = 2-2\cos(\pi/d). 
$$
Moreover, the multiplicity of the smallest eigenvalue of $\Delta^{\sigma_\pi}$ is precisely $2$, since for $t=\pi$,
$$ \mu_0(\pi) = \mu_{d-1}(\pi) < \mu_j(\pi) \quad \text{for all $j =1,2,\dots,d-2$.} $$

