% \( A_2 \) & Even under \( E \), odd under \( \sigma_x \) and \( \sigma_y \) & Antisymmetric under mirror planes \\
% \( B_1 \) & Even under \( \sigma_x \), odd under \( \sigma_y \) & Associated with \( x \)-like modes \\
% \( B_2 \) & Odd under \( \sigma_x \), even under \( \sigma_y \) & Associated with \( y \)-like modes \\
% \end{tabular}
% \caption{Irreducible representations of the \( C_{2v} \) point group with physical interpretations. \textcolor{blue}{This is quite straightforward. I don't think it deserves a separate table in the main text.}}
% \label{tab:irreps}
% \end{table}

In the far field, the electric field must be transverse, and is expressed as \( \mathbf{\Vec{E}} = (E_x, E_y) \), where \( E_x \) and \( E_y \) are the in-plane components. These components transform under the \( C_{2v} \) symmetry operations according to the \( B_1 \) and \( B_2 \) irreps, corresponding to \( x \)- and \( y \)-polarized light, respectively~\cite{tsimokha2022acoustic}. Consequently, only those eigenmodes that transform as \( B_1 \) or \( B_2 \) can couple to the corresponding polarization in the far field. Modes transforming under the \( A_1 \) or \( A_2 \) irreps are symmetry mismatched with the far-field polarization and thus remain decoupled. These uncoupled modes are known as \textbf{symmetry-protected bound states in the continuum (SP-BICs)}.

