% Model
\emph{Setup} --- We use a one-dimensional polaron model to illustrate our method~\cite{Marchand2013}; generalizations to higher dimensions will be straightforward. The Hamiltonian is $H = K+V$. $K$ describes electron hopping in a chain of $N$ sites subject to periodic boundary condition:
\begin{subequations}
\label{eq:hamiltonian}
\begin{align}
K = -\sum_{i} (c^\dagger_i c^{\phantom\dagger}_{i+1} + c^\dagger_{i+1} c^{\phantom\dagger}_i).
\end{align}
$c_i$ ($c^\dagger_i$) annihilates (creates) a spinless electron on site $i$. The hopping amplitude is set to $1$. $V$ describes the phonons and their coupling to electrons:
\begin{align}
V = \sum_{q} [ \omega_{q} b^\dagger_{q} b^{\phantom\dagger}_{q}-\lambda f_{q}n_{q} (b_{q} + b^\dagger_{-q})].
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
$b_{q}$ ($b^\dagger_{q}$) annihilates (creates) a phonon with wave vector $q$. $\omega_{q}$ is the dispersion relation. The coupling constant $\lambda>0$. The form factor $f_{q} = f^\ast_{-q}$. $n_{q} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\sum_{k} c^\dagger_{k+q} c^{\phantom\dagger}_{k}$ is the Fourier transform of electron density operator. $H$ conserves the total number of electrons $N_e = \sum_i c^\dagger_i c^{\phantom\dagger}_i$.

% propagator
The quantity of interest is the electron propagator :
\begin{align}
iG^R (k,t) = \theta(t)\langle 0|c^{\phantom\dagger}_k e^{-i H t} c^{\dagger}_k|0\rangle.
\label{eq:G_def}
\end{align}
$|0\rangle$ refers to the ground state of Eq.~\eqref{eq:hamiltonian} in $N_e = 0$ sector. $k$ is the electron wave vector. The Heaviside function $\theta(t)$ enforces the causality $t>0$.

% path integral in x
\emph{Path integral} --- We express $G^R(k,t)$ as a path integral. Trotterizing the time evolution to second order, and inserting partial resolution of identity by electron position eigenstates, we find~\cite{SM}:
\begin{subequations}
\label{eq:path_integral_x}
\begin{align}
iG^R(k,t) = \theta(t)\sum_{\{x\}} e^{ik x_M}  I(\{x\})A_0(\{x\}).
\end{align}
$M$ is the number of time slices. $\{x\} = \{x_{M},\cdots x_1,x_0\}$ are electron position at successive times. We impose the boundary condition $x_0 = 0$, whereas $x_{n\ge1}$ is unrestricted. $A_0$ is the quantum amplitude for a free electron, $A_0(\{x\}) = \prod^M_{n=1} f(x_n-x_{n-1})$, with $f(x_n-x_{n-1}) = \langle x_{n}|e^{-i\epsilon K}|x_{n-1}\rangle$. $\epsilon = t/M$ is the Trotter time.

$I$ is the phonon influence functional:
\begin{align}
I(\{x\}) = \langle \phi |e^{-i\frac{\epsilon}{2}V(x_M)}\cdots e^{-i\epsilon V(x_1)}e^{-i\frac{\epsilon}{2}V(x_0)}|\phi\rangle.
\label{eq:I_def_x}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
$|\phi\rangle$ is the phonon vacuum. Here, we have used the fact that $V$ commutes with electron coordinate $x$. $V(x)$ is the phonon Hamiltonian when the electron is located at $x$, $V(x) = \sum_q [\omega_q b^\dagger_q b^{\phantom\dagger}_q - \frac{\lambda}{\sqrt{N}} (f_q e^{iqx} b_q + h.c.)]$.

% path integral in s
We perform a change of variable from position to displacement $s_n = x_n-x_{n-1}$:
\begin{subequations}
\label{eq:path_integral_s}
\begin{align}
iG^R(k,t) = \theta(t)\sum_{\{s\}} (\prod^M_{n=1} e^{ik s_{n}}) I(\{s\})A_0(\{s\}).
\end{align}
$\{s\} = \{s_M,\cdots s_2,s_1\}$. $A_0(\{s\}) = \prod^M_{n=1} f(s_n)$. Since $|f(s)|\sim O(\epsilon^{|s|})$, we restrict the summation over $s_n$ to the range $|s_n|\le2$. The introduced error is on the same order as the Trotter error. As $\{s\}$ constitute the physical legs for the MPS, this truncation reduces the computational cost by decreasing the physical dimension.

We rewrite the phonon influence functional $I$ in terms of $\{s\}$. We define a translation operator $T(a)$ that maps the phonon mode on site $x$ to $x+a$. As a result, $V(x) = T(x) V(0) T(-x)$. Inserting it into Eq.~\eqref{eq:I_def_x} yields, 
\begin{align}
I(\{s\}) = \langle \phi| U(s_M) \cdots U(s_2) U(s_1)|\phi\rangle.
\label{eq:I_def_s}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
$U(s) = e^{-i\frac{\epsilon}{2}V(0)} T(-s) e^{-i\frac{\epsilon}{2}V(0)}$ is an $s$-dependent unitary operator. This trick is amount to a change of reference frame from the phonons to the electron, and reminiscent of the Lee-Low-Pines transformation~\cite{Lee1953}. 

% the compression problem
Eq.~\eqref{eq:path_integral_s} is our starting point. We wish to compress the quantum amplitude $A(\{s\}) \equiv I(\{s\})A_0(\{s\})$ as an MPS, $A(\{s\}) \approx A_M(s_M)\cdots A_1(s_1)$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:sketch}(b)). Then, we may evaluate the propagator as (assuming $t>0$):
\begin{align}
iG^R(k,t) \approx \sum_{s_M} e^{iks_M}A_M(s_M)\cdots \sum_{s_1} e^{iks_1}A_1(s_1),
\label{eq:contraction}
\end{align}
with complexity $O(5M\chi^2)$, $\chi$ being the bond dimension.

