\section{Choice of Authoring Tools}\label{sec:tools}

%\subsection{Choice of Software}

%There are many choices for authoring software, as well as many file formats that these softwares will output.  The following is a (non-comprehensive) list of as software systems/formats each of which offer I know some support for writing equations:

Accessible electronic documentation requires at least three different kinds of tools that need to be compatible with each other: file formats, authoring software and reading/viewing software. 

\subsection{File Formats}

The following is a list of the main document formats I have seen used for mathematical documents, each of which offer various accessibility features:

\begin{itemize}
\item HTML (the common language for webpages that has many accessibility features).  Mathematics within HTML can be displayed in different ways, such as using MathJax (an engine for displaying mathematics using images that works in all web browsers) or MathML (a standard for mathematics that is supported to varying extents by different browsers).
\item PDF (Adobe portable document format with various accessibility features)
\item ePub (an electronic-book file format based on HTML)
\item Microsoft Word/Powerpoint
\item Google Docs
\end{itemize}

My understanding is that the best accessibility features are available in HTML (and thus also in ePub).  In fact for complex formulae really HTML is the only good choice since that will allow the exploration of formulae for the visually impaired, as discussed in Section \ref{sec:alttextformulae}.


There are at least two reasons that HTML is superior for accessibility.  First, being the language of the web,  this format is most widely used and accessibility has been a feature of the web for a long time.  The second is that HTML is designed with structure in mind and then the formatting is overlaid using ``stylesheets".  This more easily allows for features that allow the user to change the page size, orientation, fonts properties etc.


By comparison, PDF was designed with a static written page in mind, and although there certainly are accessible features in PDF, the default Adobe PDF viewers and screen readers do not handle equations well.  There are existing accessibility PDF standards, and work in progress to leverage these, and hopefully in the coming years PDF will also be an option for highly accessible mathematics documents \cite{latextagging}.



\subsection{Authoring Tools}

As I expect the reader already knows, research mathematics and a lot of mathematics in higher education is written in \LaTeX.  Within this ecosystem there are a number of authoring tools available:

\begin{itemize}
\item {\LaTeX} comes in a number of variants (e.g.\ pdfLaTeX, XeLaTeX, LuaLaTeX) each of which are typically used to produce PDF files.
\item ConTeXt.  This is a less used {\TeX} based system, which is also typically used to produce PDF files (see \cite[Section 10]{mathincontext} for a discussion on how ConTeXt makes formulae accessible).
\item {\TeXml}.  This is a collection of opensource tools created and used by the American Mathematical Society to produce HTML and ePub output from LaTeX files.  More about this below.
\item \LaTeX ML.  This is a tool now being used by the arXiv to produce HTML output from uploaded {\LaTeX} research papers.
\item Pandoc. This is a tool that converts between different file formats, and can convert between {\LaTeX} and HTML.  
\end{itemize}

There are other authoring software such as Microsoft Word and PowerPoint, Google Docs as well as Adobe products to directly produce PDF files, and each of these offer some level of accessibility features.  There are also various tools that assist in composing HTML directly. 

I shall not attempt to comment on writing mathematics documents in Word, or how to make such documents accessible, other than to say that this seems possible and practical and may be a good choice especially for non-research documents and/or documents with less complicated formulae.  I hope to add more on this topic in a future version of this document.

% These will not be discussed further here, partly because I have much less familiarity with them.

%\subsubsection{Reading/Viewing Tools}
%Browsers; NVDA screeen readers; Braile machines...


\subsection{Recommendation}

 A good choice of software tools means compromising between maximizing the level of accessibility available and minimizing the amount of additional effort required by the author.  

 This compromise will depend on context, including whether one is creating a new document from scratch or adapting an existing document, the intended audience, and the complexity of the formulae the document will contain.

Here are my personal, I hope unbiased, thoughts on the subject.   Whatever the choice of tool, there will be a learning curve as authors need to use the tools in the way intended to produce accessible documents.  Much of this learning will be common to whatever choice is made.   

To make an existing document accessible it may be most convenient to use whatever tool the document was originally written in rather than rewrite the document from scratch (although depending on how the original document was created a complete rewrite may actually be faster!).

But for new documents with complicated formulae, the best results will come from combining LaTeX and HTML (using either MathML or MathJax), since that will give all the high-quality mathematical typesetting and the most advanced accessibility features.  

Conversion between LaTeX and HTML is possible through Pandoc, but this requires having documents that fit into Pandoc's internal scheme, and so will likely only work for relatively simple mathematical documents.

I am aware then of two practical solutions namely {\TeXml} and \LaTeX ML.  I do not attempt to offer any kind of comprehensive user documentation here, but instead end with a brief synopsis of what they are.  I hope to add more in a future version of this document.


\section{{\TeXml}}

I use the term {\TeXml} to refer to a suite of tools used by the publishing department of the American Mathematical Society (AMS).  These tools are used by the AMS to take research articles and books written in {\LaTeX} and convert them to either HTML or ePub format that are then served on their journal's ``MathViewer" pages (e.g. \cite{amsjournals}).

The first parts of these tools are available as opensource projects \cite{texmlgithub}.   With assistance from the AMS I and other members of my department have been testing to see how these tools can be used as a practical way to produce highly accessible HTML from {\LaTeX} source.  

These tools have some restrictions (for instance they are written for documents that use the amsart or amsbook class).  There is good support for some {\LaTeX} features and packages, and some are either not supported or need additional attention from authors.     

The only important feature that I see missing from these tools at present is the ability to add alternative text to images, that I understand is work-in-progress.    There are some other, more minor, features that might be needed if one wants to create fully WCAG compliant documents with all the things I have so far seen in ``regular" mathematics documents.

Those intending to submit their work to the AMS, or those who expect to use the amsart or amsbook class, will likely find these good tools to use.    One drawback we have faced is the challenge to install these tools locally.  Another drawback is that there is currently limited user documentation.  


\section{{\LaTeX ML}}

Another tool for converting {\LaTeX} to HTML is {\LaTeX ML} \cite{latexmlwebsite}.   This was originally created not for accessibility but in the development of the Digital Library of Mathematical Functions \cite{digitalfunctions}.  Recently the arXiv preprint server has started using this tool to provide HTML versions of papers as a way to increase accessibility in mathematical research.

{\LaTeX ML} is opensource, can easily be installed on many platforms (Linux, Mac, Windows), and there is documentation that explains how to use it and how it works internally.   It is able to directly output both HTML and ePub.

My understanding is that the goal of the {\LaTeX ML} project is to create highly structured HTML from {\LaTeX} source, which, if authors use in the right way, is likely to be highly accessible.   I am not aware of any specific documentation that describes how to best use {\LaTeX ML} to achieve the best accessibility or compliance.  

My observations are that {\LaTeX ML} already does a huge number of things that authors are likely to need to create accessible documents (including working with a wide range of {\LaTeX} styles and packages, support for equations, and the ability to add alternative text to images).  Like {\TeXml}, there are some other, more minor, features that might be needed if one wants to create fully WCAG compliant documents with all the things I have so far seen in ``regular" mathematics documents.


