We can see from equation (\ref{positive}) that  the dominant contribution to the change in entropy arises from
a positive coefficient multiplied by the sum of the perturbative shifts of the zero eigenvalues.  Since the eigenvalues of a density matrix must be non-negative,
the  perturbative shifts of the zero eigenvalues must  be non-negative and, therefore, 
the change in entropy must be non-negative.  Thus, for any initial special separable state with a nontrivial kernel, we have a direct analog of the second law of thermodynamics. If the interaction is weak enough, if the
contribution in equation (\ref{}shift of S) is non-zero, the entropy  of the subsystem is non-decreasing.
One can also apply the triangle inequality to the expression on the right-hand-side of equation (\ref{shift of S}) 
to re-confirm that $\delta S$ is non-negative. 

The first term on the right-hand-side of equation (\ref{shift of S}) has a simple interpretation.  It is the transition probability in the Born approximation, $\left|<m,\tilde m|\hat{T}^{(1)}| m',\tilde m'>\right|^2$,  for transitions from 
initial non-zero probability states $\left|m'\notin\kappa,\tilde m'\right>$ to initial zero probability states, $|m\in\kappa,\tilde m>$, weighted by the probability $\rho_{i,m',\tilde m'}$ that $\left|m'\notin\kappa,\tilde m'\right>$ occurs in the initial state The second term compensates for some over-counting.  For our example (\ref{initial example}) we see explicitly that 
\begin{align}
&\delta S=\left[\lambda^2\ln\frac{1}{\lambda^2}\right]\frac{1}{2}\biggl(
|<0,\tilde 0|\hat{T}^{(1)}| \uparrow,\tilde \downarrow>|^2
+\nonumber \\ &
|<0,\tilde 0|\hat{T}^{(1)}| \downarrow,\tilde \uparrow>|^2
+
|<0,\tilde \uparrow|\hat{T}^{(1)}| \uparrow,\tilde \downarrow>|^2
\nonumber \\ &
+
|<0,\tilde \downarrow|\hat{T}^{(1)}| \downarrow,\tilde \uparrow>|^2\biggr)
\end{align}
which is the sum over transition probabilities from initial states to states in the kernel, where
the requirement is that the state of both of the subsystems must change.  Indeed, this is essential. 
If the interactions were entirely within one subsystem, for example, 
$ <n,\tilde n|\hat{T}^{(1)}|m,\tilde m> = <n|\hat{T}^{(1)}|m> \delta_{\tilde n,\tilde m}$, the  subsystems would be isolated and the individual entropies of the subsystems could not change.  The second term in the full
expression (\ref{shift of S}) is essential for enforcing the condition that the states of both subsystems must change.  This is seen in the simple example in (\ref{simple example}). 
It is also seen for the case of a pure incoming state, where $\rho_i=|1,\tilde 1><1,\tilde 1|$, equation (\ref{shift of S}) reduces to
\begin{align}
&\delta S = \left[\lambda^2\ln\frac{1}{\lambda^2}\right] \sum_{m\neq 1,\tilde m\neq 1} 
|<m,\tilde m|\hat{T}^{(1)}|1,\tilde 1>|^2  
+\ldots
\label{shift of S for a pure state}
\end{align}
which is simply the total transition probability to all states with the proviso that the states of both of the subsystems must change. 

For pure initial states, the non-analyticity in $\lambda$ of the leading contribution to the von Neumann entropy was noted in some of the early literature on momentum space entanglement in quantum field theory\cite{vanR} although the rest of the above formula is quite different from theirs. 
Here we are assuming that $\lambda$ is sufficiently small that $\lambda^2\ln\frac{1}{\lambda^2}>>\lambda^2$. 
The corrections denoted by ellipses in  (\ref{positive}) and (\ref{shift of S}) to this dominant term are of order $\lambda^2$ and higher. 



This also gives a particularly simple expression for the entropy generated by scattering.  If, for example, for for the elastic scatting of two particles which preserves momentum, the exclusion is equation (\ref{shift of S for a pure state}) is automatic for the $\hat{T}$-matrix and the formula relates  the rate of quantum entanglement entropy buildup between the particles to the total transition probability.   When normalized to a rate  per unit of incoming beam flux the entropy buildup is equal to $ \lambda^2 \ln\left[\frac{1}{\lambda^2}\right] $ times the total  elastic two-body cross-section.   This ``area law'', $\delta S$/(time)(beam flux)$\sim \sigma_T$,  for the rate of entropy increase is already known in existing literature.  





