We will use a basis for the quantum states $|m,\tilde m>\equiv |m>\otimes|\tilde m>$ where $|m>$ and $|\tilde m>$ are
basis for the Hilbert spaces of each of the two subsystems. 
The initial reduced density matrix, which we denote by $\bar\rho_i$  is defined as the partial trace of the initial density matrix, 
\begin{align}
&\bar\rho_i \equiv \sum_{m,n} |m><n| \biggl[ \sum_{\tilde m}<m,\tilde m|\rho_{i}|n,\tilde m>\biggr]
\label{reduced initial density matrix}
\end{align}
In addition, we shall choose the states of the subsystems, $|m>$ and $|\tilde m>$,  so that
the initial reduced density matrices for each of the subsystems is diagonal, 
 \begin{align}
\sum_{\tilde m} <m,\tilde m|\rho_{i}|n,\tilde m> = \bar\rho_{i,m}\delta_{m,n} 
\end{align}
and a similar formula for the tilded subsystem.

We will make two assumptions about the initial density matrix $\rho_i$.  First, we will assume that it
is separable, that is, that its eigenvectors are products of states of the subsystems.  In the bases that we
are using, it can then be written as
\begin{align}\label{separable}
\rho_i = \sum_{m,\tilde m}~\rho_{i,m,\tilde m}~|m,\tilde m><m,\tilde m|
\end{align}
This is more general than a product state which would have $\rho_i=\bar\rho_i\otimes\tilde{\bar\rho}_i$. The latter
contains $N+\tilde N$ parameters whereas the separable state (\ref{separable}) contains $N\tilde N$ parameters where $N,\tilde N$ are the dimensions of the Hilbert spaces. 

Then, our second assumption will be that the reduced initial density matrix for one of the subsystems, 
\begin{align}
&\bar \rho_i = \sum_{m,n,\tilde m} |m><n| <m,\tilde m|\rho_i|n,\tilde m>
\nonumber \\ 
&=
\sum_{m}\bar \rho_{i,m}|m><m|~,~~
\bar\rho_{i,m}= \sum_{\tilde m} \rho_{i,m,\tilde m}
\end{align}
has a non-empty kernel, which we denote by $\kappa$, that is, there are some values of $m\in\kappa$ 
for which $\bar \rho_{i,m}=0$.  Then, separability also implies 
$\sum_{\tilde m}\rho_{i,m\in\kappa,\tilde m}=0$  and the fact that $\rho_{i,m,\tilde m}$ must be non-negative implies that $\rho_{i,m ,\tilde m}=0$, $\forall m\in\kappa$,  $\forall$ $\tilde m$.  
The latter assumption, that $\bar\rho_i$ has a kernel, is not overly restrictive.  It holds for any pure state and, amongst mixed states it only implies that there are some states with vanishing probability.  For example, in a system with only a finite amount of energy available, these could be very high energy states. 


Let us consider an isolated quantum mechanical system whose physics are governed by the Schr\"odinger
equation
\begin{align}\label{schroedinger equation}
i\hbar\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\Psi(t) = (H_0+\lambda H_I)\Psi(t)
\end{align}
where $\Psi(t)$ is the time-dependent wave-function in the Schr\"odinger picture. 
We will consider a bipartite system where the non-interacting Hamiltonian is a sum of 
Hamiltonians for the two subsystems 
\begin{align}
H_0=H_0^{(1)}+H_0^{(2)}
\end{align}
and the interaction Hamiltonian $\lambda H_I$ couples the subsystems as well as the degrees 
of freedom within the individual systems. We will assume that $\lambda$ is a 
dimensionless parameter and all of our work in the following will focus on studying dynamics in an
asympotic expansion in $\lambda$, expected to be valid only when $\lambda$ is very small. 

The density matrix of the full isolated system evolves in time by conjugation with a unitary time evolution operator
\begin{align}\label{evolution}
\rho(t_2) =U(t_2,t_1)\rho(t_1)U^\dagger(t_2,t_1)
\end{align}
The time evolution operator in the interaction picture is given by the expression
\begin{align}\label{time evolution operator}
U(t_2.t_1)=\mathcal T \exp\left(-\frac{i\lambda}{\hbar}\int_{t_1}^{t_2}dt H_I(t)\right)
\end{align}
where, $\mathcal T$ denotes time ordering and 
\begin{align}\label{interaction picture hamiltonian}
H_I(t) = e^{\frac{i}{\hbar}H_0t}H_I e^{-\frac{i}{\hbar}H_0t}
\end{align}



 For sufficiently weak coupling, the largest contribution to the entropy change will come from states for which $\bar\rho_{i,m}=0$ since, in an expansion of the right-hand-side of equation (\ref{delta S}), they contribute 
$ - \delta\bar\rho_{m} \ln\left(\delta\bar\rho_{m}\right)$
and the logarithm of  small numbers have large magnitudes. If the initial reduced density matrix has a non-empty kernel, $\delta S\sim  -\sum_{m\in\kappa} \delta\bar\rho_{i,m} \ln\left({\rm small~number}\right)$,
where we denote the set of indices labeling states in 
the kernel by $\kappa$. 
.  We will hereafter assume that  the kernel of the non-negative operator  $\bar\rho_i$  is non-empty, so that $\bar\rho_{i,m\in\kappa }=0$ and $\bar\rho_{i,m\notin\kappa}>0$. 

Finding the shift of the zero eigenvalues, $\delta\bar\rho_{m\in\kappa}$ 
due to the evolution of the reduced density matrix
 is an exercise in degenerate perturbation theory. 
 We can find the shift  $\delta\bar\rho$  of the reduced density matrix by taking the partial trace over the $|\tilde m>$-states of equation (\ref{perturbative final density matrix}), to get the expansion in $\lambda$,
 \begin{align}
 \delta\bar\rho& = \sum_{m,m'}|m><m'|\left(\sum_{\tilde m}<m,\tilde m| \delta\rho |m',\tilde m>\right)
 \nonumber 
\\&=\lambda \delta\bar\rho^{(1)} +\lambda^2 \delta\bar\rho^{(2)}+\ldots \label{delta lambda}
\end{align}
Then, given the shift, $\delta\bar\rho$ in (\ref{delta lambda}), we need to find the  shifts of the degenerate zero eigenvalues of $\bar\rho$.  To do this to the second order in degenerate perturbation theory, we must find the eigenvalues of the matrix
\begin{align}
&\lambda<m|\delta\bar\rho^{(1)}|m'>+\lambda^2<m|\delta\bar\rho^{(2)}|m'>
\nonumber
\\ &~+\lambda^2 \sum_{m''\notin\kappa}<m| \delta\bar\rho^{(1)}|m''>\frac{1}{\bar\rho_{i,m''} }<m''|\delta\bar\rho^{(1)} |m'>+\ldots
\label{matrix}
\end{align}
  for states $|m>$ and $|m'>$ with $m,m'\in\kappa$.  The ellipses in equation (\ref{matrix}) stand for contributions
  of order $\lambda^3$ and higher.


We shall only need the sum of the eigenvalues of the  matrix in (\ref{matrix}), which is its trace.
 In-putting 
equation (\ref{perturbative final density matrix}), we find the following expression 
\begin{align}
&\sum_{m\in\kappa}\delta\bar\rho_{m} =
\sum_{m\in\kappa}\sum_{\tilde m}\biggl(i\lambda <m,\tilde m|\left[  \hat{T}^{(1)},\rho_i\right] |m,\tilde m>
\nonumber \\ & 
+  i\frac{\lambda^2}{2}<m,\tilde m|\left[
 \hat{T}^{(2)}+\hat{T}^{(2)^\dagger},\rho_i\right]|m,\tilde m>
 \nonumber \\ & 
- \frac{\lambda^2}{2} <m,\tilde m|\left[\hat{T}^{(1)},\left[  \hat{T}^{(1)}, \rho_i\right]\right]|m,\tilde m> \biggr)
\nonumber \\ &
+ \sum_{m\in\kappa,m'\notin\kappa}   \lambda^2  \frac{ |\sum_{\tilde m}<m,\tilde m| \left[\hat{T}^{(1)},\rho_i\right] |m',\tilde m>|^2}{\bar\rho_{i,m} }
\nonumber \\ &
+\ldots
\label{shift of the kernel}
\end{align}
where the ellipses stand for contributions of order $\lambda^3$ and higher. 

For a generic initial density matrix, it is possible that the first term
on the right-hand-side of equation (\ref{shift of the kernel}) is non-zero, in which case it will be the dominant contribution.  It
is also possible that it is either negative or positive, and as a consequence, the entropy can either increase or decrease.  In fact, nothing in our formalism so far fixes the sign of $\lambda$ so a linear term in $\lambda$ can have either sign. 
We will narrow our possible choices of initial states by assuming that, for a generic
Hermitian matrix $\hat{T}^{(1)}$, the first term in (\ref{shift of the kernel}) vanishes. This is so if the
initial density matrix has the property
\begin{align}
\left[ \rho_i ,\Pi_\kappa \right]=0~,~~\Pi_\kappa= \sum_{m\in\kappa,\tilde m}|m,\tilde m><m,\tilde m|
\label{constraint}
\end{align}
that the initial density matrix commutes with the projection operator $\Pi_\kappa$. We can
decompose decompose such an initial density matrix as
\begin{align}
\rho_i=\rho_{i\kappa}+\rho_{i\not\kappa}~,~
\rho_{i\kappa}=\Pi_\kappa\rho_i ,~
\rho_{i\not\kappa}=(\mathbb{1}-\Pi_\kappa)\rho_i 
\end{align}

Let us assume that the initial density matrix $\rho_i$ has the property that $\bar\rho_i$ has a nontrivial kernel and
that it obeys equation (\ref{constraint}). Then, in fact, the first two terms on the right-hand-side of equation (\ref{shift of the kernel}) vanish and we find that the change of the entropy is
\begin{align}
&\delta S =  \lambda^2\ln\left[\frac{1}{\lambda^2}\right]\sum_{ n\in\kappa} \delta\bar\rho_{i, n}
\nonumber \\
&=  \lambda^2\ln\left[\frac{1}{\lambda^2}\right]\biggl( {\rm Tr}\left[\hat{T}^{(1)},\left[  \hat{T}^{(1)}, \rho_i\right]\right]
\Pi_\kappa \biggr)
\nonumber \\ &
+ \sum_{m\in\kappa,m'\notin\kappa}   \lambda^2  \frac{ |\sum_{\tilde m}<m,\tilde m| \left[\hat{T}^{(1)},\rho_i\right] |m',\tilde m>|^2}{\bar\rho_{i,m} }
\nonumber \\ &
+\ldots
\label{entanglement generated}
\end{align} 

\begin{align}
&\delta S =  \lambda^2\ln\left[\frac{1}{\lambda^2}\right]\sum_{ n\in\kappa} \delta\bar\rho_{i, n}
\nonumber \\
&=  \lambda^2\ln\left[\frac{1}{\lambda^2}\right]\biggl( {\rm Tr}\biggl[
\hat{T}^{(1)}\hat{T}^{(1)}\rho_{i\not\kappa}\biggr]
\biggr)
\nonumber \\ &
+ \sum_{m\in\kappa,m'\notin\kappa}   \lambda^2  \frac{ |\sum_{\tilde m}<m,\tilde m| \left[\hat{T}^{(1)},\rho_i\right] |m',\tilde m>|^2}{\bar\rho_{i,m} }
\nonumber \\ &
+\ldots
\label{entanglement generated}
\end{align} 
