We will make two assumptions about the initial state of the bipartite system. 
Firstly, 
we will focus on a particular class of initial states which are separable, 
in the sense that the initial density matrix can we written in the form
\begin{align}\label{separable initial state}
\rho_i = \sum_{m,\tilde m} \rho_{i,m,\tilde m}|m,\tilde m><m,\tilde m|
\end{align}
where $|m,\tilde m>\equiv|m>\otimes|\tilde m>$ are direct products of orthonormal states of each of the two subsystems. Being a density matrix, the eigenvalues $ \rho_{i,m,\tilde m}$ of $\rho_i$ must be real numbers, $ 0\leq \rho_{i,m,\tilde m}\leq1$
and $\sum_{m,\tilde m}\rho_{i,m,\tilde m}=1$.

The fact that the eigenvectors of $\rho_i$ are such products is the definition of a separable state. It is more
general than a product state where $\rho_i=\bar\rho_i\otimes\tilde{\bar \rho}_i$ and where the
eigenvalues would have the form $\rho_{i,m,\tilde m}=\bar\rho_{i,m}\tilde{\bar \rho}_{i,\tilde m}$.  The separable state has $N_1\times N_2$ parameters whereas the product state has $N_1+N_2$ parameters where $N_1,N_2$ are the dimensions of the subsystem Hilbert spaces. 

Then, our second assumption will be that the reduced initial density matrix for one of the subsystems, 
\begin{align}
&\bar \rho_i = \sum_{m,n,\tilde m} |m><n| <m,\tilde m|\rho_i|n,\tilde m>
\nonumber \\ 
&=
\sum_{m}\bar \rho_{i,m}|m><m|~,~~
\bar\rho_{i,m}= \sum_{\tilde m} \rho_{i,m,\tilde m}
\end{align}
has a non-empty kernel, which we denote by $\kappa$, that is, there are some values of $m\in\kappa$ 
for which $\bar \rho_{i,m}=0$.  Then, separability also implies 
$\sum_{\tilde m}\rho_{i,m\in\kappa,\tilde m}=0$  and the fact that $\rho_{i,m,\tilde m}$ must be non-negative implies that $\rho_{i,m ,\tilde m}=0$, $\forall m\in\kappa$,  $\forall$ $\tilde m$.  
The latter assumption, that $\bar\rho_i$ has a kernel, is not overly restrictive.  It holds for any pure state and, amongst mixed states it only implies that there are some states with vanishing probability.  For example, in a system with only a finite amount of energy available, these could be very high energy states. 

Since  $\rho_i$  evolves in time by the unitary conjugation in equations (\ref{evolution}) and (\ref{s matrix}), the eigenvalues of the full density matrix $\rho_i$ are independent of time, that is, $\rho_f$ and $\rho_i$ must have identical eigenvalues. However, this is not so for the reduced density matrix. 
%\begin{align}
%\bar\rho_i \to \bar\rho_f+\delta\bar\rho~,~
%\delta\bar\rho=\lambda\delta\bar\rho^{(1)}+\lambda^2\delta\bar\rho^{(2)}+\ldots
%\end{align}
  The time evolution of its eigenvalues will determine the change of the entropy of the subsystems with time, 
\begin{align}
&\delta { S} = -{\rm Tr}[\bar\rho_f\ln\bar\rho_f ] + {\rm Tr}[\bar\rho_i\bar \ln\rho_i ]
\nonumber \\
 &= -\sum_n \biggl[\left(\bar\rho_{i,n}+ \delta\bar\rho_{i,n}\right)\ln\left(\bar\rho_{i,n}+ \delta\bar\rho_{i,n}\right)
 -\bar\rho_{i,n}\ln\bar\rho_{i,n}\biggr] %\label{approximate entropy increase}
\nonumber
\end{align}
From this expression, we can see that, for sufficiently weak coupling (small magnitude of $\lambda$), the largest contribution to the entropy change will come from states for which $\bar\rho_{i,n}=0$, since they contribute 
$ - \delta\bar\rho_{i,n} \ln\left(\delta\bar\rho_{i,n}\right)$
and the logarithm of  small numbers have large magnitudes. If the kernel of $\bar\rho_i$ is nontrivial, these will be the dominant contributions. The leading contribution to the density matrix will be
$S\sim  -\sum_{n\in\kappa} \delta\bar\rho_{i,n} \ln\left({\rm small~number}\right)$ where $\kappa$ is the kernel of $\bar\rho_i$.

 Finding the shift of the zero eigenvalues due to the evolution of the reduced density matrix
 is an exercise in degenerate perturbation theory. 
 We can find the shift  $\delta\bar\rho$  of the reduced density matrix by taking the partial trace over the $|\tilde n>$-states of equation (\ref{perturbative final density matrix}),
 \begin{align}
 \delta\bar\rho& = \sum_{k,\ell}|k><\ell|~\sum_{\tilde n}<k,\tilde n| \delta\rho |\ell,\tilde n>
 \nonumber 
\\&=\lambda \delta\bar\rho^{(1)} +\lambda^2 \delta\bar\rho^{(2)}+\ldots \label{delta lambda}
\end{align}
Then, given the shift of $\delta\bar\rho_i$ in (\ref{delta lambda}) we need to find the  shifts of its degenerate zero eigenvalues.  To do this to the second order in degenerate perturbation theory, we must find the eigenvalues of the matrix
\begin{align*}
&\lambda<n|\delta\bar\rho^{(1)}|n'>+\lambda^2<n|\delta\bar\rho^{(2)}|n'>
\\ &~~~~~~~~~+\lambda^2 \sum_{n''\notin\kappa}<n| \delta\bar\rho^{(1)}|n''>\frac{1}{\bar\rho_{i,n''} }<n''|\delta\bar\rho^{(1)} |n'>+\ldots
\end{align*}
  for states $|n>$ and $|n'>$, with $n,n'\in\kappa$. 
In fact, we only need the sum of the eigenvalues which is the trace of this matrix. 
 Inputting 
equation (\ref{perturbative final density matrix}), we find that 
\begin{align}
&\sum_{n\in\kappa}\delta\bar\rho_{i,n} =
\sum_{n\in\kappa}\sum_{\tilde n}\biggl(i\lambda <n,\tilde n|\left[  \hat{T}^{(1)},\rho_i\right] |n,\tilde n>
\nonumber \\ & 
+  i\frac{\lambda^2}{2}<n,\tilde n|\left[
 \hat{T}^{(2)}+\hat{T}^{(2)^\dagger},\rho_i\right]|n,\tilde n>
 \nonumber \\ & 
- i\frac{\lambda^2}{2} <n,\tilde n|\left[\hat{T}^{(1)},\left[  \hat{T}^{(1)}, \rho_i\right]\right]|n,\tilde n> \biggr)
\nonumber \\ &
+ \sum_{n\in\kappa,m\notin\kappa}   \lambda^2  \frac{ |\sum_{\tilde m}<m,\tilde m| \left[\hat{T}^{(1)},\rho_i\right] |n,\tilde m>|^2}{\bar\rho_{i,m} }
\nonumber \\ &
+\ldots
\label{shift of the kernel}
\end{align}
where the ellipses stand for contributions of order $\lambda^3$ and higher. 

Now, we can see that, if the initial density matrix is separable, the first two terms on the right-hand-side of equation (\ref{shift of the kernel}) vanish.  Theright-hand-side is then at least of order $\lambda^2$ and the dominant term 
is
\begin{align}\label{entanglement generated1}
\delta S =  \lambda^2\ln\left[\frac{1}{\lambda^2}\right]\sum_{ n\in\kappa} \delta\bar\rho_{i, n}
\end{align} 
For pure initial states, the non-analyticity in $\lambda$ of the leading contribution to the von Neumann entropy was noted in some of the early literature on momentum space entanglement in quantum field theory\cite{vanR}.
We note that  $\sum_{ n\in\kappa} \delta\bar\rho_{i, n}+\sum_{ n\notin\kappa} \delta\bar\rho_{i, n}=0$, and $\sum_{ n\in\kappa} \delta\bar\rho_{i, n}\geq 0$ since they are perturbations of zero eigenvalues and the eigenvalues must always be positive.
\cite{Low:2024hvn} see that
\begin{align}\label{second law}
\delta S\geq 0
\end{align}
The entropy of a separable initial state with a nontrivial kernel must always increase if the coupling is weak enough.

The significance of separability of the initial state is also clear.  The first two terms on the right-hand-side
of equation (\ref{shift of the kernel}) are guaranteed to vanish for a separable $\rho_i$ but they can be nonzero for a non-separable $\rho_i$.  Moreover, there is noting intrinsic to the quantum theory which fixed their sign, in fact we have not specified the sign of $\lambda$ so there is no fundamental reason why the entropy of a non-separable state could not decrease. It would have to be a non-separable state for which one of the first two terms on the right-hand-side of (\ref{shift of the kernel}) is nonzero. 

In summary, our result for the change of entropy is 
\begin{align}
&\delta S =  \lambda^2 \ln\left[\frac{1}{\lambda^2}\right]\biggr( \sum_{ n\in\kappa,\tilde n}
\sum_{m\notin\kappa,\tilde m}\rho_{i,m,\tilde m}
\left| <n,\tilde n|\hat{T}^{(1)}|m,\tilde m> \right|^2
\nonumber \\ &
- \sum_{n\in\kappa,m\notin \kappa}     \frac{ |\sum_{\tilde m}\rho_{i,m,\tilde m}<m,\tilde m| \ \hat{T}^{(1)} |n,\tilde m>|^2}{\sum_{\tilde m}\rho_{i,m,\tilde m} }\biggr)+\ldots
\label{entanglement generated}
\end{align}

If the initial state were a product state, so that $\rho_{i,m,\tilde m}=\rho_{i,m}\tilde\rho_{i,\tilde m}$, 
equation (\ref{}) would have the form 
\begin{align}
&\delta S =  \lambda^2 \ln\left[\frac{1}{\lambda^2}\right]\sum_{n\in\kappa,m\notin\kappa}\rho_{im} \biggr( \sum_{\tilde n,
\tilde m}\tilde\rho_{i,\tilde m}
\left| <n,\tilde n|\hat{T}^{(1)}|m,\tilde m> \right|^2
\nonumber \\ &
- \sum_{n\in\kappa,m\notin \kappa}     \frac{ |\sum_{\tilde m}\rho_{i,m,\tilde m}<m,\tilde m| \ \hat{T}^{(1)} |n,\tilde m>|^2}{\sum_{\tilde m}\rho_{i,m,\tilde m} }\biggr)+\ldots
\label{entanglement generated}
\end{align}
One can use the triangle inequality to re-confirm that the right-hand-side of equation (\ref{entanglement generated}) is non-negative. 
The first term on the right-hand-side of equation (\ref{entanglement generated}
) has a simple interpretation.  It is the transition probability in the Born approximation for transitions from 
initial non-zero probability states to initial zero probability states, weighted by the initial probabilities. This contribution is already known in existing literature \cite{seki1}\cite{seki2}\cite{us1}\cite{Grignani:2016igg}.  The second term, on the other hand is novel and it arises from careful treatment of second order perturbation theory. Its role is to enforce some exclusions.   For example, if the interactions were entirely within one subsystem so that, for example, 
$ <n,\tilde n|\hat{T}^{(1)}|m,\tilde m> = <n|\hat{T}^{(1)}|m> \delta_{\tilde n,\tilde m}$, the individual entropies of the subsystems should not change.  The second term is easily seen to cancel the first term in that case. 



Our considerations for the von Neumann entropy are easily generalized to the Tsallis entropy which is defined as
\begin{align}\label{tsallis}
S_k = \frac{1}{k-1}\left( 1-{\rm Tr}\bar\rho^k\right)
\end{align}
The von Neumann entropy is a limit of the Tsallis entropy, $S=\lim_{k\to 1}S_k$. 
Using the same reasoning as above, we can easily find that, for a separable initial state where the initial density matrix has a non-empty kernel,  the leading contribution to the Tsallis entropy, when $k>1$,  is a similar expression to that in equation (\ref{entanglement generated}) but with a different coefficient
\begin{align}
&\delta S_k=
 \frac{k\lambda^2}{k-1} \biggr( \sum_{ n\in\kappa,\tilde n}\biggl|
\sum_{m\notin\kappa,\tilde m}\rho_{i,m,\tilde m}
<n,\tilde n|\hat{T}^{(1)}|m,\tilde m> \biggr|^2
\nonumber \\ &
- \sum_{n\in\kappa,m\notin \kappa}     \frac{ |\sum_{\tilde m}\rho_{i,m,\tilde m}<m,\tilde m| \ \hat{T}^{(1)} |n,\tilde m>|^2}{\sum_{\tilde m}\rho_{i,m,\tilde m} }\biggr)+\ldots
\label{result for  tsalis entropy}
\end{align}
This expression is more accurate for small $\lambda$ for large $k$ and is valid only when $k>1$. To obtain this expression, we have used the fact that the trace of the reduced density matrix must be preserved by the time evolution, 
$$\sum_{n\notin\kappa,\tilde n}\bar\rho_{i,n,\tilde n}+\sum_{n\in\kappa,\tilde n}\bar\rho_{i,n,\tilde n}=0$$
 This allows us to write the right-hand-side of equation (\ref{result for  tsalis entropy}) in a form where the term in brackets is identical to the term in brackets in equation  (\ref{entanglement generated}) for the von Neumann entropy. 

The expression in equation (\ref{entanglement generated}) for the entropy generated is particularly simple in the case of a pure initial state, say where $\rho_{i,m,\tilde m}=\delta^1_m\delta^1_{\tilde m}$,  
\begin{align}
&\delta S =  \lambda^2 \ln\left[\frac{1}{\lambda^2}\right] \sum_{n\neq 1, \tilde n\neq 1}\left|
<n,\tilde n|\hat{T}^{(1)}|1,1> \right|^2+\ldots
\label{pure state entanglement generated}
\end{align}
which is proportional to the total transition probability to states other than the initial state with the only
constraint that the states of both subsystems must change.  
This also gives a particularly simple expression for the entropy generated by scattering.  If, for example, particles in a given in-coming plane-wave state scatter from a fixed target the rate of quantum entanglement entropy buildup between the particles and the target per unit time and per unit of incoming beam flux is simply equal to $ \lambda^2 \ln\left[\frac{1}{\lambda^2}\right] $ times the total  cross-section.   

