Since density matrix eigenvalues must be positive, the perturbed zero eigenvalues, $\delta\bar\rho_{\alpha'}$ must be non-negative numbers.  However, they are not easy to compute as they are the eigenvalues of the matrix
$
<\alpha'|\delta\bar\rho|\beta'>
$ where 
\begin{align}\label{perturbative final reduced density matrix}
\delta\bar\rho&=\sum_{\tilde\alpha}<\tilde\alpha|\left[ i\lambda  \hat{T}_1  +
i\frac{\lambda^2}{2} \left( \hat{T}_2+\hat{T}_2^\dagger\right) ,\rho_i\right]
-\frac{\lambda^2}{2}\left[  \hat{T}_1,\left[ \hat{T}_1, \rho_i\right]\right]
 |\tilde\alpha>
+\ldots
\end{align}

 We can, however, use the fact that all of the eigenvalues of the density matrix must sum to one, so the corrections must sum to zero,
\begin{align}\label{sum rule}
\sum_\alpha\delta\bar\rho_\alpha + \sum_{\alpha'}\delta\bar\rho_\alpha'=0
\end{align}
Indeed, the sum over all of the eigenvalues of $\delta\bar\rho$ is equal to its trace and, from equation (\ref{perturbative final density matrix}) or equation (\ref{perturbative final reduced density matrix}) we can easily see that the trace of the operator $\delta\rho$ over the entire space of quantum states vanishes. Its eigenvalues must therefore sum to zero, as written in equation (\ref{sum rule}).

Moreover, every eigenvalue of the density matrix must be non-negative.  This means that the perturbations of the zero eigenvalues, if they are non-zero, must be positive, 
\begin{align}
\delta\bar\rho_{\alpha'}\geq 0~\to~\sum_{\alpha'}\delta\bar\rho_{\alpha'}\geq 0
\end{align}
Combined with equation (\ref{sum rule}), this implies that
\begin{align}
\sum_\alpha \delta\bar\rho_\alpha<0
\end{align}

which we can use in equation (\ref{delta s}) to get
 \begin{align}\label{delta s}
S_f&=S_i   -\ln(1/\lambda^2)\sum_{\alpha}\delta\bar\rho_{\alpha}+\ldots \\
\end{align}
which shows that this term in the change of the entropy is always positive -- for small enough $\lambda$ the entanglement entropy is always non-decreasing  for any interaction -- as long as perturbation theory is valid.    All we have
used here is the assumption that the initial reduced density matrix does not have full rank, but has
some zero eigenvalues. 


\begin{align}
\sum_\alpha\delta\bar\rho_\alpha = \sum_{\alpha}<\alpha| \delta\bar\rho |\alpha> + 
\sum_{\alpha, \beta\neq\alpha}\frac{|<\alpha|\delta\bar\rho|\beta>|^2}{\bar\rho_{i\alpha}-\bar\rho_{i\beta} }
+\sum_{\alpha, \beta' }\frac{|<\alpha|\delta\bar\rho|\beta'>|^2}{\bar\rho_{i\alpha} }
\end{align}
The second term in the equation above vanishes due to the fact that the numerator is symmetric under $\alpha\leftrightarrow\beta$ and the denominator is antisymmetric. The final expression is then
\begin{align}
&\sum_\alpha\delta\bar\rho_\alpha = \sum_{\alpha}<\alpha| \delta\bar\rho |\alpha> + 
\sum_{\alpha, \beta' }\frac{|<\alpha|\delta\bar\rho|\beta'>|^2}{\bar\rho_{i\alpha} }
\nonumber \\ &
=
\sum_{\alpha,\tilde\alpha}<\alpha|<\tilde\alpha|\left[ i\lambda  \hat{T}_1  +
i\frac{\lambda^2}{2} \left( \hat{T}_2+\hat{T}_2^\dagger\right) ,\rho_i\right]
-\frac{\lambda^2}{2}\left[  \hat{T}_1,\left[ \hat{T}_1, \rho_i\right]\right]|\alpha>|\tilde\alpha>
\nonumber \\ &
+
\sum_{\alpha, \beta' }\frac{|\sum_{\tilde\alpha}<\alpha|<\tilde\alpha|\left[ i\lambda  \hat{T}_1 
,\rho_i\right]|\beta'>|\tilde\alpha>|^2}{\bar\rho_{i\alpha} }
+\ldots
\end{align}


Now, let us assume that the initial state is separable.  This means that its density matrix is the product of density matrices for the two subsystems.   In particular this means that our decomposition of the system into two subsystems is preserved by diagonalization of the density matrix, and that the eigenvectors are unentangled products of states for each subsystem.  Then, we can choose the states $|\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha>\equiv|\alpha^+>\otimes |\tilde\alpha>$ and $|\alpha+0,\tilde\alpha>\equiv |\alpha+0>\otimes|\tilde\alpha>$ as eigenvectors of the incoming density matrix $\rho_i$.   The states $|\alpha^+>$  and $|\alpha^0>$ are also eigenvectors of the reduced incoming density matrix $\bar\rho_i$ with positive and zero eigenvalues, respectively.  Here we are using the notation $|\alpha^+>$ for eigenvectors with non-zero eigenvalues of $\bar\rho_i$, the eigenvalue is denoted by $\bar\rho_{i\alpha^+}$,  and $|\alpha^0>$ for eigenvectors which are in the kernel of $\bar\rho_i$.  Generally, 
$$
\bar\rho_{i\alpha^+}=\sum_{\tilde\alpha}\rho_{i\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha}~,~
0=\sum_{\tilde\alpha}\rho_{i\alpha^0,\tilde\alpha}
$$

When the states that we are taking matrix elements of are eigenvectors of $\rho_i$, the diagonal matrix elements of any commutator of an operator with $\rho_1$ must vanish and, in particular, the matrix elements of the first, commutator term on the right-hand-side of the above equation vanishes.  The equation simplifies to 
\begin{align}
&\sum_{\alpha^+}\delta\bar\rho_{\alpha^+} = \sum_{\alpha^+}<\alpha^+| \delta\bar\rho |\alpha^+> + 
\sum_{\alpha^+, \alpha^0}\frac{|<\alpha^+|\delta\bar\rho|\alpha^0>|^2}{\bar\rho_{i\alpha^+} }
\nonumber \\ &
=
-\frac{\lambda^2}{2}\sum_{\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha}<\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha|
\left[  \hat{T}_1,\left[ \hat{T}_1, \rho_i\right]\right]|\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha>
%\nonumber \\ &
+\lambda^2 
\sum_{\alpha^+, \alpha^0 }\frac{|\sum_{\tilde\alpha}<\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha |\left[ \hat{T}_1 
,\rho_i\right]|\alpha^0,\tilde\alpha>|^2}{\bar\rho_{i\alpha^+} }
+\ldots
\label{delta rho}\end{align}

Now, since 
$$
\left[  \hat{T}_1,\left[ \hat{T}_1, \rho_i\right]\right]=
 \hat{T}_1\left[ \hat{T}_1, \rho_i\right]-\left[ \hat{T}_1, \rho_i\right]\hat{T}_1
 =\hat{T}_1^2\rho_i -2\hat{T}_1\rho_i\hat{T}_1+\rho_i\hat{T}_1^2
$$
$$
\sum_{\tilde\alpha}<\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha|
\left[  \hat{T}_1,\left[ \hat{T}_1, \rho_i\right]\right]|\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha>
=\sum_{\tilde\alpha}<\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha|
\hat{T}_1^2\rho_i -2\hat{T}_1\rho_i\hat{T}_1+\rho_i\hat{T}_1^2|\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha>
$$
$$
=2\sum_{\tilde\alpha,\beta^+,\tilde\beta}|<\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha|T_1|\beta^+,\tilde\beta>|^2
[\rho_{i\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha}-\rho_{i\beta^+,\tilde\beta}]
+
2\sum_{\tilde\alpha,\beta^0,\tilde\beta}|<\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha|T_1|\beta^0,\tilde\beta>|^2
[\rho_{i\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha}-\rho_{i\beta^0,\tilde\beta}]
$$
We can also rewrite the last term in equaiton (\ref{delta rho}) as 
$$
\sum_{\alpha^+, \alpha^0 }\frac{|\sum_{\tilde\alpha}<\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha|\left[ \hat{T}_1 
,\rho_i\right]|\alpha^0,\tilde\alpha>|^2}{\bar\rho_{i\alpha^+} }
=
\sum_{\alpha^+, \alpha^0 }\frac{|\sum_{\tilde\alpha}<\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha|\hat{T}_1 
|\alpha^0,\tilde\alpha> (\rho_{i\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha} - \rho_{i\alpha^0,\tilde\alpha})|^2}{\bar\rho_{i\alpha^+} }
$$
$$
=
\sum_{\alpha^+, \alpha^0, \tilde\alpha,\tilde\beta}  
<\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha|\hat{T}_1 
|\alpha^0,\tilde\alpha>  
<\alpha^0,\tilde\beta|\hat{T}_1 
|\alpha^+,\tilde\beta>\frac{  (\rho_{i\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha} - \rho_{i\alpha^0,\tilde\alpha})
(\rho_{i\alpha^+,\tilde\beta} - \rho_{i\alpha^0,\tilde\beta})
 }{\bar\rho_{i\alpha^+}}
$$
Combining these observations, we have
\begin{align}
&\sum_{\alpha^+}\delta\bar\rho_{\alpha^+} =\lambda^2\sum_{\tilde\alpha,\tilde\beta}\biggl[
\sum_{\alpha^+, \alpha^0}  
<\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha|\hat{T}_1 
|\alpha^0,\tilde\alpha>  
<\alpha^0,\tilde\beta|\hat{T}_1 
|\alpha^+,\tilde\beta>\frac{  (\rho_{i\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha} - \rho_{i\alpha^0,\tilde\alpha})
(\rho_{i\alpha^+,\tilde\beta} - \rho_{i\alpha^0,\tilde\beta})
 }{\bar\rho_{i\alpha^+}}
\nonumber \\ & -\sum_{\beta^+}|<\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha|T_1|\beta^+,\tilde\beta>|^2
[\rho_{i\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha}-\rho_{i\beta^+,\tilde\beta}]
\nonumber \\ & -
\sum_{\beta^0}|<\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha|T_1|\beta^0,\tilde\beta>|^2
[\rho_{i\alpha^+,\tilde\alpha}-\rho_{i\beta^0,\tilde\beta}] \biggr]
