Let us assume that we can write the states in the Hilbert space as superpositions of basis vectors which
are products of the states of two subsystems $|a>|\tilde a>$.  The perturbative evolution of the density
matrix is given in time-dependent perturbation theory
\begin{align}
\rho=\rho_{i}+\delta\rho =\rho_{i} - i[\lambda \hat{T},\rho_{i}]
\end{align}
where the ``T-matrix'' $\lambda \hat{T}$ is given by in time dependent perturbation theory by the time integral of the
interaction Hamiltonian in the interaction picture,
\end{align}
\lambda \hat{T}=\frac{1}{\hbar}\int_{t_i}^{t_f} dt~ H_I(t)
\end{align}
We have extracted a coupling constant from this quantity.  It is assumed to be a small real dimensionless number. 
$ \hat{T}$ is an operator which we will assume has finite matrix elements in the space of states.  The validity of the approximation that we are making in first order perturbation theory is controlled by the small magnitude of $\lambda$. 

 We shall assume that the basis if a Hilbert space can be decomposed into a direct product of two basis, one for an ``accessible system'' whose states we call $|a>$ and one for an ``inaccessible' system'' whose states are $|\tilde a>$.  States of the composite of the accessible and inaccessible systems are the product states  $|a>|\tilde a>$

All of the operators above are matrices acting on this space.  For example
\end{align}
\rho_{a\tilde a b\tilde b}=<a|<\tilde a|\rho |b>|\tilde b>~,~~
\rho_{ia\tilde a b\tilde b}=<a|<\tilde a|\rho_i |b>|\tilde b>~,~~
\lambda \hat{T}_{a\tilde a b\tilde b}=<a|<\tilde a|\lambda \hat{T} |b>|\tilde b>
\end{align}

The reduced density matrix is obtained by tracing over the tilded degrees of freedom to get 
\end{align}
\bar\rho_{ab} = \sum_{\tilde a}\rho_{a\tilde a b\tilde a}~,~~
\bar\rho_{iab} = \sum_{\tilde a}\rho_{ia\tilde a b\tilde a}
\end{align}
With these definitions, 
\end{align}
 \bar\rho_{ab} =\bar\rho_{iab}+\delta\bar\rho_{ab}
\end{align}
\end{align}
\delta \bar\rho_{ab} =- i~\sum_{\tilde ac\tilde c}  \lambda \hat{T}_{a\tilde a c\tilde c}\rho_{ic\tilde c b\tilde a}
 + i~\sum_{\tilde ac\tilde c}  \rho_{ia\tilde a c\tilde c}\lambda \hat{T}_{c\tilde c b\tilde a}
\end{align}


Let us assume that we can choose the $|a>$ basis so that $\bar\rho_i$ is diagonal, that is
\end{align}
\bar\rho_{iab} = \rho_{ia} \delta_{ab} 
\end{align}
with the eigenvalues being probabilities of finding the various quantum states when measurements are performed,
\end{align}
0\leq \rho_a\leq 1~,~~\sum_a\rho_a=1
\end{align}

Then the eigenvalues of $\bar\rho_{ab}$ can be found by perturbation theory.  
Let us assume that $\rho_i^{\rm red}$ has some zero and some non-zero eigenvalues.  That is, we further decompose the span of the $|a>$-states into superpositions of two kinds of basis vectors $\{ |a'>, |a''>\}$. 
so that 
$  |a'>$ are eigenvectors of $\bar\rho_i$ with nonzero eigenvalues and $|a''>$ are in the kernel of $\bar\rho_i$. 

Then the non-zero eigenvalues are
perturbed as
\end{align}
\rho_{a'}\to \rho_{ia'}+ \delta\rho_{a'a'}= \rho_{ia'}
- i~\sum_{\tilde a'c\tilde c} \lambda \hat{T}_{ a'\tilde a c\tilde c}\rho_{ic\tilde c a'\tilde a}
 + i~\sum_{\tilde ac\tilde c}  \rho_{ia'\tilde a c\tilde c}\lambda \hat{T}_{c\tilde c a'\tilde a}
\end{align}

The zero eigenvalues are found by diagonalizing the matrix
\end{align}
\delta\rho_{a''c''}=
- i~\sum_{\tilde a c\tilde c}  \lambda \hat{T}_{a''\tilde a c\tilde c}\rho_{ic\tilde c c'' \tilde a}
 + i~\sum_{\tilde ac\tilde c}  \rho_{ia''\tilde a c\tilde c}\lambda \hat{T}_{c\tilde c c''\tilde a}
\end{align}
Note that the eigenvalues of this matrix must all be of order $\lambda$. 

Now, let us form the von Neumann entropy of the reduced density matrix
\end{align}
S=-{\rm Tr} \bar\rho\ln \bar\rho=-{\rm Tr} [\bar\rho_i+\delta\rho]\ln [\bar\rho_i
+\delta\rho]
\end{align}
Now, if we take the trace in the basis where $\rho_i^{\rm red}$ is diagonal, we see that the contribution
of the zero eigenspace of $\rho_i^{\rm red}$ is of order $\lambda\ln\lambda$ whereas the nonzero eigenspace
is of order $\lambda$.  Since $\lambda$ is a small number, parts proportional to $\lambda\ln\lambda$ are dominant. The leasing correction is thus gotten from the part of the trace which sums over the $|a''>$ states, \
\end{align}
S=-{\rm Tr} \bar\rho_i\ln\bar\rho_i
-{\rm Tr}''\delta\rho\ln\delta\rho
\end{align}
\end{align}
=-{\rm Tr} \bar\rho_i\ln\bar\rho_i
- \ln\lambda{\rm Tr}''\delta\rho 
\end{align} 
The correction to the von Neumann entropy is then
\end{align}
\delta S = -i\lambda \ln\lambda \sum_{a'' \tilde a  c\tilde c}  
\left[ \hat{T}_{a''\tilde a c\tilde c}\rho_{ic\tilde c a'' \tilde a}
 -~   \rho_{ia''\tilde a c\tilde c}\hat{T}_{c\tilde c ca''\tilde a}
\right]
\end{align}
Note that, amongst the $a$ indices, if this quantity were summed over both $a'$ and $a''$ it would vanish.
Thus we could trade the $a''$ sum for an $a'$ sum and an overall minus sign. 
\end{align}
\delta S = i\lambda \ln\lambda \sum_{a' \tilde a  c\tilde c}  
\left[ \hat{T}_{a'\tilde a c\tilde c}\rho_{ic\tilde c a' \tilde a}
 -~   \rho_{ia'\tilde a c\tilde c}\hat{T}_{c\tilde c ca'\tilde a}
\right]
\end{align}
The example where $\rho_i$ is that of a pure state and we use a basis where that state is $|1>$ gives
\end{align}
\delta S = i\lambda \ln\lambda \sum_{a' \tilde a  c\tilde c}  
\left[ \hat{T}_{a'\tilde a c\tilde c}\rho_{ic\tilde c a' \tilde a}
 -~   \rho_{ia'\tilde a c\tilde c}\hat{T}_{c\tilde c ca'\tilde a}
\right]
\end{align}
