The raw datasets $\mathcal{B}_\text{LLM}$ may contain low-quality or invalid outputs. Therefore, we apply a filtering mechanism to retain a high-quality subset $\mathcal{B}_{\text{fil}}$. To weed out low-quality samples, we check whether the yielded target region $g_{i}^{*}$ belongs to the target set $ \mathcal{T}$, the length of the inference content $\mathscr{I}_i^{*}$ is illegal with anomalous symbols, and it conforms to the task constraints based on the $r_i$. Mathematically,
\begin{equation}
\label{QualityEvaluation}
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Check}\left(g_i^{*}, \mathscr{I}_{i}^{*}, R\right) \\
& = 
 \mathbb{I}\left(g_i^{*} \in \mathcal{G}_a\right) \cdot w_{1,g} + \mathbb{I}\left(\mathscr{I}_{i}^{*} \cap \Lambda = \emptyset\right) \cdot w_{2,g} \\
& + \mathbb{I}\left(L_{\min} \leq\left|\mathscr{I}_{i}^{*}\right| \leq L_{\max}\right) \cdot w_{3,g}  + \mathbb{I}\left(R \geq \tau_r\right) \cdot w_{4,g},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbb{I}\left(\cdot\right)$ is an indicator function that returns $1$ when the condition is established and $0$ otherwise; $w_{1,g}$, $w_{2,g}$, $w_{3,g}$, $w_{4,g}$ are the indicator importance weights; $\Lambda$ is a pre-defined set of anomalous characters; $L_{\min}$ and $L_{\max}$ denote the minimum and maximum inference result lengths, respectively.

