		\title{The Neural Networks with Tensor Weights and the Corresponding Fermionic Quantum Field Theory}
		\date{\today}

		\author{Guojun Huang}
		\address{The School of Science and Engineering, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Shenzhen}
		\affiliation{Physics Department, Tsinghua University, Beijing 100084, China}
		\author{Kai Zhou}
		\address{The School of Science and Engineering, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Shenzhen}
		\begin{abstract}
			In this paper, we establish a theoretical connection between complex-valued neural networks (CVNNs) and fermionic quantum field theory (QFT), bridging a fundamental gap in the emerging framework of neural network quantum field theory (NN-QFT). While prior NN-QFT works have linked real-valued architectures to bosonic fields, we demonstrate that CVNNs equipped with tensor-valued weights intrinsically generate fermionic quantum fields. By promoting hidden-to-output weights to Clifford algebra-valued tensors,  we induce anticommutation relations essential for fermionic statistics. Through analytical study of the generating functional, we obtain the exact quantum state in the infinite-width limit, revealing that
            %study the complex-valued neural network (CVNN) with the physical concept, generating functional; analytically, the quantum state for infinite width hidden layer condition is obtained. 
            the parameters between the input layer and the last hidden layer correspond to the eigenvalues of the quantum system,  and the tensor weighting parameters in the hidden-to-output layer map to dynamical fermionic fields. The continuum limit reproduces free fermion correlators, with diagrammatic expansions confirming anticommutation. The work provides the first explicit mapping from neural architectures to fermionic QFT at the level of correlation functions and generating functional. It extends NN-QFT beyond bosonic theories and opens avenues for encoding fermionic symmetries into machine learning models, with potential applications in quantum simulation and lattice field theory.%Finally, the fermionic quantum field is constructed by transforming these weighting parameters into tensor-formatted coefficients.
		\end{abstract}
		\maketitle
		\section{Introduction}

		%Previous researches point out that for a pile of Neural Networks, with their parameters randomly initialized as independent Gaussian variables, a correspondence to the quantum field theory (NN-QFT) can be constructed
        Neural networks with random Gaussian weights have been rigorously connected to free bosonic quantum field theories, forming a one-to-one correspondence now known as neural-network quantum field theory (NNQFT)~\cite{Halverson:2020trp,Grosvenor:2021eol,Dyer:2019uzd,Yaida:2019sjo,Bachtis:2021xoh,Zavatone-Veth:2021ogo,Maiti:2021fpy,Roberts:2021fes,Demirtas:2023fir,Erdmenger:2021sot,Erbin:2021kqf,Garcia-Martin:2023sgi,Erbin:2022lls,Demichev:2022qnl}. For standard feed-forward architectures real-valued parameters, it has been shown~\cite{Halverson:2020trp,Yaida:2019sjo} that the $n$-th order correlation function of the network output can be expressed as a sum over bosonic $n$-points Feynman diagrams,
		\begin{eqnarray} 
			&&G^{(n)}_{\rm NN}({\bm x}_1,{\bm x}_2,...,{\bm x}_n)\\
			&\equiv&\frac{1}{n_{\rm nets}}\sum_{\alpha=1}^{n_{\rm nets}}f_{\alpha}({\bm x}_1)f_{\alpha}({\bm x}_2)\cdots f_{\alpha}({\bm x}_n),\nonumber
		\end{eqnarray}
		where $f_{\alpha}({\bm x}_n)$ denotes the network output evaluated at the space-time position ${\bm x}_{n}$ for a particular realisation of the randomly sampled weights $\alpha$. For the single-hidden-layer architecture, it can be written as
		\begin{eqnarray}
			f_{\alpha}({\bm x}_{j})&=&\sigma({\bm x}_{j}W_{\rm in,h}^{(\alpha)}+{b}_{\rm h}^{(\alpha)})W_{\rm h,out}^{(\alpha)},
		\end{eqnarray}
        where $W_{\rm in,h}$ and $W_{\rm h,out}$ are, respectively, the input-to-hidden and hidden-to-output weight matrices, $b_{h}$ is the bias of the hidden layer, the subscripts $h$ label hidden neurons while superscripts $\alpha$ label independent network realisations, and $\sigma$ is the element-wise activation function. In this work we adopt the exponential activation \cite{Halverson:2020trp}
        %\begin{eqnarray}
		%\sigma(z)&=&\frac{\exp(z)}{\exp\left(\sigma_{\rm b}^{2}+\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}{\bm x}_{j}^{2}/{D}\right)}.
		%\end{eqnarray}
		%and the excitation function $\sigma(x)$ being
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\sigma({\bm x}_{j}W_{\rm in,h}+{b}_{\rm h})&=&\frac{\exp({\bm x}_{j}W_{\rm in,h}+{b}_{\rm h})}{\exp\left(\sigma_{\rm b}^{2}+\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}{\bm x}_{j}^{2}/{D}\right)},
		\end{eqnarray}
        which normalizes the mean pre-activation to zero under the Gaussian weight and bias ensemble. 
		%The input ${\bm x}_{j}$ is the ${D}$-dimensional space-time ${\bm x}_{j}=(x_{j}^{(1)},x_{j}^{(2)},...,x_{j}^{({D})})$ with $j=1,2,...,n$. The parameters $W_{\rm in,h}$, $W_{\rm h,out}$ are the weights between input layer and hidden layer, hidden layer and output layer, respectively, the parameter ${\bm b}_{h}$ is the bias of the hidden layer.


        Pushing the hidden-layer width $H$ to infinity collapses the diagrammatic expansion to its Gaussian core: all interaction vertices are suppressed by factors of $\mathcal{O}(1/H)$, so the network's effective Lagrangian reduces to that of a free scalar field and only the two-point propagator survives~\cite{Garcia-Martin:2023sgi}. In this asymptotic regime the model is mathematically equivalent to a Gaussian process, yet the precise quantum state that emerges from the weight ensemble--the field configuration measure that reproduces these correlators--has not been fully characterized.

		%In the limit of infinite hidden layer width, the Lagrangian of the network field $f_{\alpha}({\bm\alpha})$ becomes a Gaussian process, which is actually a free field theory, with its interaction term being depressed by the inverse hidden layer width $1/H$~\cite{Halverson:2020trp}. However, the corresponding quantum state for infinite width condition is not clear.

        When the hidden layer is finite yet parametrically wide, $H\gg 1$, the leading $1/H$ corrections to the free Lagrangian organize themselves into a low-energy effective theory~\cite{Yaida:2019sjo}. A systematic Taylor expansion of the activation kernel reveals that the first non-vanishing interaction is quartic, so the network's dynamics are governed by a $\phi^4$ term whose coupling scales as $\mathcal{O}(H^{-1})$. Because the hidden-to-output weights $W_{\rm h,out}$ are drawn from an even distribution, all odd powers cancel, leaving this quartic vertex as the dominant interaction mode.

		%As for finite but large enough width hidden layer condition, the Lagrangian of $f$ can always be approximated as $\phi^{4}$ theory via Tayler expansion~\cite{Demirtas:2023fir}, since the distribution of $W_{\rm h,out}$ is even function and the lowest order of $1/H$ contribution to the Lagrangian of $f$ shall be the $f^{4}$ term.

        For neural networks with genuinely narrow hidden layers, the $\phi^4$ approximation fails: higher-order vertices proliferate and the diagrammatic expansion of the $n$-point correlators becomes markedly intricate. In this regime, the weight ensemble generates an entire hierarchy of interaction terms beyond quartic order, rendering perturbative treatments intractable.% and necessitating a fully non-perturbative analysis.

		%However, for narrow width hidden layer condition, the $\phi^{4}$ theory fails to describe the neural network, and the Feynman diagrams for the $n$-pt correlation function becomes complicated~\cite{Halverson:2020trp}.

		The bosonic nature of the field $f$ is determined by the distribution of $W_{\rm h,out}$, to which the i.i.d. real-Gaussian distribution can induce the non-interaction Bose field. The problem arises when transforming the condition from bosonic to fermionic field, since the Lagrangian of fermionic field can not simply be connected to the distribution. 
        Replacing each scalar weight component by tensor-valued coefficients~\cite{Du:2025atv} that form a basis of Clifford generators, $\{\gamma_h\}$, forces every weight component to anticommute with every other. This single architectural change converts the Gaussian process that arises at $H\to\infty$ from Bose to Fermi statistics while leaving the activation kernel, the diagrammatic rules and the large-$H$ power counting untouched. Complex-valued networks provide the minimal setting for such tensor weights, and are thus prospective to be connected to fermionic field theory.
        %The complex-valued neural network is prospective to connect to the fermionic field theory. 
        While previous works focused on bosonic fields, here we establish the first explicit neural-network - fermionic QFT mapping that works at the level of correlation functions and generating functionals. %mappling to fermionic QFT.

		%Previous progresses about fermionic system 
        Earlier work on machine learning fermionic systems has focused on using neural networks to represent many-body wavefunctions of a prescribed Hamiltonian~\cite{Choo:2020byz,Kim:2023fwy,Lovato:2022tjh,Liu:2023cke,Cassella:2022boh,Moreno:2021jas}. Here we tackle the complementary questions posed by NN-QFT: \textbf{what neural architecture gives rise \textit{intrinsically} to a fermionic quantum field theory?} %what construction should a neural network be when its corresponding quantum field being a fermionic field? Or is there one kind of neural network that its corresponding quantum field should be fermionic field? 
        We show that complex-valued networks equipped with Clifford-tensor output weights answer this question positively--providing, in effect, a learnable lattice of Grassmann variables whose continuum limit reproduces free fermion correlators. This closes a conceptual gap in the NN-QFT programme, extending it beyond the bosonic domain explored to date. %The main purpose of this paper is to study the complex-valued neural network and figure out its construction which will connect to the fermionic quantum field theory.
        This work also joins a growing synergy between physics and machine learning, where neural networks have been applied to challenges in physics (e.g., condensed matter physics\cite{PhysRevB.102.041124,doi:10.1126/science.aag2302,PhysRevB.94.195105,PhysRevLett.122.080602,CiCP-23-3} and high energy nuclear physics\cite{Zhou:2023pti,Jiang:2021gsw,Wang:2023exq,Zhou:2018ill,doi:10.1073/pnas.1912789117}). Conversely, physical principles have inspired novel machine learning developments\cite{Aarts:2024rsl,Chen:2022ytr} as well, demonstrating rich cross-disciplinary enrichment.

		The rest of the paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{CVNN-QFT} derives the generating functional of complex-valued neural networks and interprets it as a path integral for a complex scalar field. % via genarating functional and explain it with complex scalar field theory.
        Section~\ref{QS_infinite_H} obtains the exact quantum state for the infinite hidden layer width limit, identifying the input-to-hidden parameters as eigenvalues and the hidden-to-output parameters as dynamical fields. 
        %neural networks is obtained and the result shows that the parameters between the input layer and the last hidden layer can be regarded as the eigenvalues and the weighting parameters between the last hidden layer and the output layer should be treated as the quantum fields, therefore, in s
        Section~\ref{fermi_condition} promotes those dynamical fields to Clifford tensors, thereby realizing a fermionic theory and demonstrating how anticommutation arises diagrammatically. %we construct the fermionic field by adding anticommuting matrices to these weighting parameters, and the corresponding function $f$ turns out to be the fermionic field. Finally, section~\ref{summary}is the summary.
        Finally, section~\ref{summary} summarizes the results and outlines future directions.

		\section{Generating functional of CVNN}\label{CVNN-QFT}
        To extend the NN-QFT correspondence into the complex domain, we upgrade every parameter of the network to the complex plane. In the resulting
		complex-valued neural network (CVNN)~\cite{chen2022analysis,9849162,barrachina2023theory,bassey2021surveycomplexvaluedneuralnetworks,trabelsi2018deepcomplexnetworks,DRAMSCH2021104643,NEURIPS2023_4ac4365b,VOIGTLAENDER202333}, each weight and bias decomposes into statistically independent real and imaginary parts, both sampled from zero-mean Gaussian ensemble~\cite{Halverson:2020trp}:% can be obtained directly by replacing the real-valued parameters with complex ones, then initialize the real and imaginary parts of the weighting parameters and bias with Gauss distribution
		\begin{eqnarray}
			&&{\rm Re}(W^{(\alpha)}_{\rm in,h}),\ {\rm Im}(W^{(\alpha)}_{\rm in,h})\sim\mathcal{N}(\mu_{\rm w},\sigma_{\rm w}/\sqrt{{D}}),\\
			&&{\rm Re}(W^{(\alpha)}_{\rm h,out}),\ {\rm Im}(W^{(\alpha)}_{\rm h,out})\sim\mathcal{N}(\mu_{\rm w},\sigma_{\rm w}/\sqrt{H}),\\
			&&{\rm Re}(b^{(\alpha)}_{\rm h}),\ {\rm Im}(b^{(\alpha)}_{\rm h})\sim\mathcal{N}(\mu_{\rm b},\sigma_{\rm b}).
		\end{eqnarray}
        Ensemble averages over these Gaussians can be interpreted as a discrete path integral in the hidden-unit index $h$: the product over $h=1,...,H$ forms a lattice of ``time slices'', and in the continuum limit $H\to\infty$ it recovers the usual time-ordered functional integral that underlies quantum field theory.
		%the expectation for the correlation function can be regarded as the path integral along the $h$ steps from $h=1$ to $h=H$. The $h$ plays the role of time in path integral.

		\subsection{The path integral explanation}
		To access the $n$-points correlation function, we couple the network to an auxiliary source field $J({\bm x})$ and work with a generating functional. In the condition that the $f_{\alpha}$ is a scalar field, one can denote $W_{\rm in,h}$, $b_{\rm h}$ and $W_{\rm h,out}$ with ${\bm Q}_{h}$, ${V}_{h}$ and $\varphi_{h}$ as
		\begin{eqnarray}
			{\bm Q}_{h}&\equiv&W_{\rm in,h},\\
			{V}_{h}&\equiv&b_{\rm h},\\
			\varphi_{h}&\equiv&H*W_{\rm h,out},
		\end{eqnarray}
		with the dimension of vector ${\bm Q}_{h}$ bing $D$. Then the output function of the CVNN becomes
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\label{f_P_V_x}
			f({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})&=&\frac{1}{H}\sum_{h=1}^{H}\lambda_{h}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})\varphi_{h},\\
			\label{lambda_P_V_x}
			\lambda_{h}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})&\equiv&\frac{\exp\left({\bm x}\cdot{\bm Q}_{h}+{V}_{h}\right)}{\exp\left(\sigma_{\rm b}^{2}+\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}{\bm x}^{2}/{D}\right)},
		\end{eqnarray}
        where $\lambda_h$ plays the role of a hopping amplitude between the hidden-unit ``sites'' indexed by $h$ and continuum coordinate $x$.
		The generating functional is therefore written as a path integral~\cite{Peskin:1995ev}:
		\begin{eqnarray}\label{Z_original}
			&&Z[J^{*},J]\\
			&=&\prod_{h=1}^{H}\bigg\{\int{\rm d}{\bm Q}_{h}{\rm d}{\bm Q}_{h}^{*}{\rm d}{V}_{h}{\rm d}{V}_{h}^{*}{\rm d}\varphi_{h}{\rm d}\varphi_{h}^{*}\nonumber\\
			&&\times\frac{{\rm e}^{-||{\bm Q}_{h}||^{2}/(2\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}/{D})}}{(2\pi\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}/{D})^{{D}}}\frac{{\rm e}^{-|{V}_{h}|^{2}/(2\sigma_{\rm b}^{2})}}{2\pi\sigma_{\rm b}^{2}}\frac{{\rm e}^{-|\varphi_{h}|^{2}/(2\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}H)}}{2\pi\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}H}\bigg\}\nonumber\\
			&&\times{\rm e}^{{\rm i}\int{\rm d}^{{D}}{\bm x}\ [J({\bm x})f^{*}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})+J^{*}({\bm x})f({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})]},\nonumber
		\end{eqnarray}
		the correlation functions of $f$ can be obtained via calculating the functional derivatives of the auxiliary field $J({\bm x})$ and $J^{*}({\bm x})$. The $\varphi_{h}$ integration is easy to handle for being the linear combination, according to the integral formula that
		\begin{eqnarray}\label{integral_formula}
			{\rm e}^{-2\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}|C|^{2}/H}&=&\int\frac{{\rm d}\varphi_{\rm R}{\rm d}\varphi_{\rm I}}{2\pi\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}H}{\rm e}^{-(\varphi_{\rm R}^{2}+\varphi_{\rm I}^{2})/(2\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}H)}\nonumber\\
			&&\times{\rm e}^{{\rm i}C(\varphi_{\rm R}-{\rm i}\varphi_{\rm I})/H+{\rm i}C^{*}(\varphi_{\rm R}+{\rm i}\varphi_{\rm I})/H}.
		\end{eqnarray}
		The generating functional then can be written as the expectation over the ${\bm Q}_{h}$, $V_{h}$ distribution as
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\label{vertex_term}
			Z[J^{*},J]&=&\mathbb{E}_{{\bm Q},V}\bigg[\exp\bigg(\frac{2\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}}{H}\sum_{h=1}^{H}\tilde{S}_{h}({\bm Q},V)\bigg)\bigg],
		\end{eqnarray}
		where we denote the effective action $\tilde{S}_{h}({\bm Q},V)$ as
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\tilde{S}_{h}({\bm Q},V)&\equiv&-\int{\rm d}^{D}{\bm x} {\rm d}^{D}{\bm y}\ J({\bm x}) \mathcal{M}_{h}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x},{\bm y})J^{*}({\bm y}),\nonumber\\
		\end{eqnarray}
		with the kernel being defined as $\mathcal{M}_{h}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x},{\bm y})\equiv\lambda_{h}^{*}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})\lambda_{h}({\bm Q},V|{\bm y})$. When considering the wide width hidden layer condition, one can divide the generating functional by different $h$, the powers of $\tilde{S}_{h}({\bm Q},V)$ term can be expanded by orders of $1/H$ as 
		\begin{eqnarray}
			Z[J^{*},J]%&&\prod_{h=1}^{H}\mathbb{E}_{{\bm Q},V}\left[\exp\left(\frac{2\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}}{H}\tilde{S}_{h}({\bm Q},V)\right)\right]\\
			&=&\prod_{h=1}^{H}\bigg\{\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{m!}\bigg(\frac{2\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}}{H}\bigg)^{m}\mathbb{E}_{{\bm Q},V}[(\tilde{S}_{h}({\bm Q},V))^{m}]\bigg\}.\nonumber\\
		\end{eqnarray}
		Considering that the expectation $\mathbb{E}_{{\bm Q},V}[(\tilde{S}_{h}({\bm Q},V))^{m}]$ gives the same value for arbitrary $h$, one can replace it with the value at $h=1$ as $\mathbb{E}_{{\bm Q},V}[(\tilde{S}_{1}({\bm Q},V))^{m}]$. Then, the generating functional can be simplified as
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\label{partitial_function}
			Z[J^{*},J]&=&\prod_{h=1}^{H}Z_{h}[J^{*},J]=(Z_{1}[J^{*},J])^{H},
		\end{eqnarray}
		with $Z_{1}[J^{*},J]$ being
		\begin{widetext}
			\begin{eqnarray}
				\label{Z_1}
				&&Z_{1}[J^{*},J]\\
				&=&1+\sum_{m=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^{m}}{m!}\frac{(2\sigma_{\rm w}^{2})^{m}}{H^{m}}\int\prod_{i=1}^{m}[{\rm d}^{D}{\bm x}_{i}{\rm d}^{D}{\bm y}_{i}]\bigg[\prod_{j=1}^{m}J({\bm x}_{j})\bigg]\mathbb{E}_{{\bm Q},V}\bigg[\prod_{k=1}^{m}\mathcal{M}_{1}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x}_{k},{\bm y}_{k})\bigg]\bigg[\prod_{l=1}^{m}J^{*}({\bm y}_{l})\bigg].\nonumber
			\end{eqnarray}
		\end{widetext}
		The coefficients of the expansion actually represents the Feynman diagrams, and it can be noticed that the formation of the above generating functional is not the traditional version, $Z[J^{*},J]\sim{\rm e}^{{\rm i}W[J^{*},J]}$, with $W[J^{*},J]$ being the connect generating functional. One can still use the formula $W[J^{*},J]=-{\rm i}\ln Z[J^{*},J]$ to calculate the connect generating functional, however, the result is not simple for narrow width hidden layer condition, but in the wide width hidden layer condition, one can keep the lowest contribution beyond Gaussian process, and obtain the effective connect generating functional.

		\subsection{The connected generating functional}
        In the wide-width regime ($H\gg 1$) the product over hidden units factorises, and the familiar limitation formula
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\lim_{H\rightarrow\infty}(1+\lambda/H)^{H}&=&{\rm e}^{\lambda},
		\end{eqnarray}
        allows us to resum the leading contributions. Expanding the single-unit effective action $(\tilde S_1 (Q,V))$ in powers of the source and then applying the limit gives the cumulant series
		%in the wide width hidden layer condition, $H\gg1$, roughly, one can estimate constant $\lambda$ as
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\lambda = \sum_{m=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{m!}\frac{(2\sigma_{\rm w}^{2})^{m}}{H^{m-1}}\mathbb{E}_{{\bm Q},V}[(\tilde{S}_{1}({\bm Q},V))^{m}],
		\end{eqnarray}
		so that, to quartic order in the source, the connected generating functional can then be approximated as
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\label{partitial_function_Z}
			&&\ln Z[J^{*},J]\\
			&\approx&-2\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}\int{\rm d}^{{D}}{\bm x}\int{\rm d}^{{D}}{\bm y}\ J({\bm x}){\rm e}^{-\frac{\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}}{{D}}({\bm x}-{\bm y})^{2}}J^{*}({\bm y})\nonumber\\
			&&+\frac{2\sigma_{\rm w}^{4}}{H}\int{\rm d}^{D}{\bm x}_{1}\int{\rm d}^{D}{\bm x}_{2}\int{\rm d}^{D}{\bm y}_{1}\int{\rm d}^{D}{\bm y}_{2}\nonumber\\
			&&\times J({\bm x}_{1})J({\bm x}_{2})\Lambda({\bm x}_{1},{\bm x}_{2},{\bm y}_{1},{\bm y}_{2})J^{*}({\bm y}_{1})J^{*}({\bm y}_{2})\nonumber
		\end{eqnarray}
		with the quartic kernel $\Lambda({\bm x}_{1},{\bm x}_{2},{\bm y}_{1},{\bm y}_{2})$ being
		\begin{eqnarray}
			&&\Lambda({\bm x}_{1},{\bm x}_{2},{\bm y}_{1},{\bm y}_{2})\\
			&\equiv&\mathbb{E}_{{\bm Q},V}\big[\mathcal{M}_{1}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x}_{1},{\bm y}_{1})\mathcal{M}_{1}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x}_{2},{\bm y}_{2})\big].\nonumber
		\end{eqnarray} 
		This approximation shows that, once the hidden layer is merely parametrically wide, the leading connected diagrams organise into an effective $\phi^4$ theory whose coupling is suppressed by $1/H$. This formulation is more transparent for CVNN-QFT than attempting to approximate the Lagrangian directly at quartic order, because it keeps track of the finite-width correctons that distinguish the network from its strict $H\to\infty$ Gaussian-process limit. %generating functional can be estimate as a $\phi^{4}$-theory version, this version seems to be more suitable for the CVNN-QFT rather than estimate the Lagrangian as a $\phi^{4}$ theory.


