		\section{The quantum state in the infinite-width limit}\label{QS_infinite_H}
		In this section, we study the infinite-width hidden layer NN, $H\!\to\!\infty$, and extract the corresponding quantum state of the network in this limit. The $h$ summation can be regarded as the $H$-times sampling of the Gassian distributions ${\bm Q}_{h}\sim\mathcal{N}({\bm 0},\sigma_{\rm w}/\sqrt{D})$ and $V_{h}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\rm b})$, then in the infinite-width hidden layer limit, the summation becomes the expectation of ${\bm Q}$ and $V$ with respect to their distributions, which via the Riemann summation method can be written as a Riemann integral over a dummy variable $\xi\in[0,1]$:%, the $h$-summation can be transformed into integration as
		\begin{eqnarray}
			f_{\infty}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})&=&\int_{0}^{1}{\rm d}\xi\frac{{\rm e}^{{\bm x}\cdot{\bm Q}(\xi)+{V}(\xi)}}{{\rm e}^{\sigma_{\rm b}^{2}+\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}{\bm x}^{2}/{D}}}\varphi(\xi),
		\end{eqnarray}
		the action of $f$ becomes the free field action, because the vertexes higher than 4-points (including) vanish for measuring zero. For example, denote operator $\hat{O}_{h}({\bm x})\equiv |\lambda_{h}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})|^{2}=\lambda_{h}^{*}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})\lambda_{h}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})$, the 4-points correlation function gives
		\begin{comment}
			\begin{eqnarray}
				\big<\hat{N}({\bm x})^{2}\big>&=&8\sigma_{\rm w}^{4}\left\{\mathbb{E}_{{\bm Q},V}\big[\hat{O}(1,{\bm x})\big]\right\}^{2}+\frac{8\sigma_{\rm w}^{4}}{H}\left\{\mathbb{E}_{{\bm Q},V}\big[\hat{O}(1,{\bm x})\hat{O}(1,{\bm x})\big]-\left[\mathbb{E}_{{\bm Q},V}\big[\hat{O}(1,{\bm x})\big]\right]^{2}\right\},
			\end{eqnarray}
		\end{comment}
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\big<|f({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})|^{4}\big>&=&8\sigma_{\rm w}^{4}\left\{\mathbb{E}_{{\bm Q},V}\big[\hat{O}_{1}({\bm x})\big]\right\}^{2}\\
			&&+\frac{8\sigma_{\rm w}^{4}}{H}\mathbb{E}_{{\bm Q},V}\big[(\Delta\hat{O}_{1}({\bm x}))^{2}\big],\nonumber
		\end{eqnarray}
		with the fluctuation operator defined as $\Delta\hat{O}_{h}({\bm x})=\hat{O}_{h}({\bm x})-\mathbb{E}_{{\bm Q},V}[\hat{O}_{h}({\bm x})]$, this shows that the quantum fluctuations are depressed by the measuring zero under the limitation $H\rightarrow\infty$. This shows that $f_{\infty}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})$ has the Gaussion like distribution, with its variance being the familiar neural-network kernel
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\mathbb{E}_{{\bm Q},V}\left[\lambda_{\xi}^{*}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})\lambda_{\xi}({\bm Q},V|{\bm y})\right]&=&{\rm e}^{-\frac{\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}}{D}({\bm x}-{\bm y})^{2}}.
		\end{eqnarray}
		Since the higher order correlation functions vanish in the $H\rightarrow\infty$ limit, we can absorb the arithmetic square root of ${\bm Q}(\xi)$ and $V(\xi)$'s distributions into the definition of the field and write an equivalent path-integral representation, so that the 2-pt correlation function of $f$ equivalently reproduces the expectation of $\big<f^{*}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})f({\bm Q},V|{\bm y})\big>$,
		\begin{eqnarray}
			f_{\rm eff}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})&=&\int_{0}^{1}{\rm d}\xi\frac{{\rm e}^{-|{V}(\xi)|^{2}/(4\sigma_{\rm b}^{2})}}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma_{\rm b}^{2}}}\frac{{\rm e}^{-||{\bm Q}(\xi)||^{2}/(4\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}/{D})}}{(2\pi\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}/{D})^{{D/2}}}\nonumber\\
			&&\times\frac{\exp\left({\bm x}\cdot{\bm Q}(\xi)+{V}(\xi)\right)}{\exp\left(\sigma_{\rm b}^{2}+\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}{\bm x}^{2}/{D}\right)}\varphi(\xi),
		\end{eqnarray} 
		it can be simplified as
		\begin{eqnarray}
			&&f_{\rm eff}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})\\
			&=&\int_{0}^{1}{\rm d}\xi\frac{{\rm e}^{-|{V}(\xi)-2\sigma_{\rm b}^{2}|^{2}/(4\sigma_{\rm b}^{2})-\frac{1}{2}[V^{*}(\xi)-V(\xi)]}}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma_{\rm b}^{2}}}\nonumber\\
			&&\times\frac{{\rm e}^{-||{\bm Q}(\xi)-2\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}{\bm x}/D||^{2}/(4\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}/{D})-\frac{1}{2}{\bm x}\cdot[{\bm Q}^{*}(\xi)-{\bm Q}(\xi)]}}{(2\pi\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}/{D})^{{D/2}}}\varphi(\xi),\nonumber
		\end{eqnarray}
		the $V^{*}(\xi)-V(\xi)$ term has no effect for they vanishes in the Wick contraction of mutually conjugate fields $\left<f_{\rm eff}^{*}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})f_{\rm eff}({\bm Q},V|{\bm y})\right>$, besides, the integral of $V(\xi)$ makes no difference considering its integral transformation $V(\xi)\rightarrow \tilde{V}(\xi)\equiv V(\xi)+2\sigma_{\rm b}^{2}$ for arbitrary ${\bm x}$, namely, the parameter $V(\xi)$ can be ignored and $f_{\rm eff}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})$ can be equivalently written as
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\tilde{f}_{\rm eff}({\bm Q}|{\bm x})&=&\int_{0}^{1}{\rm d}\xi\frac{{\rm e}^{-\frac{[{\bm Q}_{\rm R}(\xi)-2\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}{\bm x}/D]^{2}+{\bm Q}_{\rm I}(\xi)^{2}}{4\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}/{D}}+{\rm i}{\bm x}\cdot{\bm Q}_{\rm I}(\xi)}}{(2\pi\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}/D)^{{D/2}}}\varphi(\xi).\nonumber\\
		\end{eqnarray}
		The Wick contraction of $\varphi(\xi)$ actually controls the equivalence of ${\bm Q}(\xi)$, and one can transform $\varphi$ to the function of ${\bm Q}$ with its 2-pt correlation function gives the Dirac delta function of ${\bm Q}$, namely, 
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\varphi(\xi)\rightarrow\tilde{\varphi}({\bm Q}),
		\end{eqnarray}
		with $\left<\tilde{\varphi}^{*}({\bm Q})\tilde{\varphi}({\bm Q}')\right>=2\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}\delta^{(D)}({\bm Q}-{\bm Q}')$. Then the field $f$ in the $H\rightarrow\infty$ can be represented by the summation of the Gaussian wave as
		\begin{eqnarray}
			&&\tilde{f}_{\rm eff}({\bm x})\\
			&=&\int\frac{{\rm d}^{D}{\bm Q}_{\rm R}{\rm d}^{D}{\bm Q}_{\rm I}}{(2\pi\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}/D)^{{D/2}}}{\rm e}^{-\frac{({\bm Q}_{\rm R}-2\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}{\bm x}/D)^{2}}{4\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}/{D}}}{\rm e}^{-\frac{{\bm Q}_{\rm I}^{2}}{4\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}/{D}}+{\rm i}{\bm x}\cdot{\bm Q}_{\rm I}}\tilde{\varphi}({\bm Q}).\nonumber
		\end{eqnarray}
		It can be deduced from the above expression that the physical understanding of the GP field should be the combination of the space-time representation Gaussian waves and the momentum representation Gaussian wave packets, considering the variables' transformations ${\bm Q}_{\rm R}\rightarrow2\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}{\bm y}/D$ and ${\bm Q}_{\rm I}\rightarrow{\bm q}$, the quantum field $\tilde{f}_{\rm eff}$ can be written as the direct product of the two Gaussian wave packets as
		\begin{comment}
			\begin{eqnarray}
				\tilde{f}_{\rm eff}({\bm x})&=&2\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}\int\frac{{\rm d}^{D}{\bm y}}{(\pi D/(2\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}))^{{D/4}}}{\rm e}^{-\frac{\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}}{D}({\bm x}-{\bm y})^{2}}\hat{\kappa}({\bm y})\nonumber\\
				&&\otimes\int\frac{{\rm d}^{D}{\bm q}}{(2\pi\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}/D)^{{D/4}}}{\rm e}^{-\frac{{\bm q}^{2}}{4\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}/{D}}+{\rm i}{\bm x}\cdot{\bm q}}\hat{\eta}({\bm q}),
			\end{eqnarray}
		\end{comment}
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\tilde{f}_{\rm eff}({\bm x})&=&\sqrt{2}\sigma_{\rm w}\int{\rm d}^{D}{\bm y}\ \psi_{{\bm p}={\bm0}}(2^{-1}\sqrt{D}/\sigma_{\rm w},{\bm x},{\bm y})\hat{\kappa}({\bm y})\nonumber\\
			&&\otimes\int{\rm d}^{D}{\bm q}\ \psi_{{\bm x}}(\sigma_{\rm w}/\sqrt{D},{\bm q},{\bm0})\hat{\eta}({\bm q}),
		\end{eqnarray}
		with $\left<\hat{\kappa}({\bm x})\hat{\kappa}({\bm y})\right>=\delta^{(D)}({\bm x}-{\bm y})$, $\left<\hat{\eta}({\bm p})\hat{\eta}({\bm q})\right>=\delta^{(D)}({\bm p}-{\bm q})$, and the wave function of a Gaussian wave packet being~\cite{Olivares_2012}
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\psi_{{\bm p}}(\sigma,{\bm x},{\bm x}_{0})&\equiv&\frac{1}{(2\pi\sigma^{2})^{D/4}}{\rm e}^{-\frac{({\bm x}-{\bm x}_{0})^{2}}{4\sigma^{2}}-{\rm i}{\bm p}\cdot({\bm x}-{\bm x}_{0})},\\
			\psi_{{\bm x}}(\sigma,{\bm q},{\bm q}_{0})&\equiv&\frac{1}{(2\pi\sigma^{2})^{D/4}}{\rm e}^{-\frac{({\bm q}-{\bm q}_{0})^{2}}{4\sigma^{2}}+{\rm i}{\bm x}\cdot({\bm q}-{\bm q}_{0})}.
		\end{eqnarray}
		There exist a Fourier transformation relation that
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\hat{\kappa}({\bm y})&=&\int\frac{{\rm d}^{D}{\bm q}}{(2\pi)^{D/2}}{\rm e}^{-{\rm i}{\bm y}\cdot{\bm q}}\hat{\eta}({\bm q}),\\
			\psi_{{\bm p}={\bm0}}\left((2\sigma)^{-1},{\bm x},{\bm y}\right)&=&\int\frac{{\rm d}^{D}{\bm q}}{(2\pi)^{D/2}}{\rm e}^{-{\rm i}{\bm y}\cdot{\bm q}}\psi_{{\bm x}}(\sigma,{\bm q},{\bm0}),\nonumber\\
		\end{eqnarray}
		then both parts of the direct product are the same Gaussian wave packets, with the real part of ${\bm Q}$ being the space coordinate $2\sigma_{\rm w}^{2}{\bm y}/D$ while its imaginary part of ${\bm Q}$ being the momentum ${\bm q}$. The network's infinite-width quantum state is therefore a tensor product of identical Gaussian wave packets in phase space, consistent with the kernel derive above.



		\section{Fermionic extension of NN-QFT}\label{fermi_condition}
        We now promote the network's hidden-to-output weights to \emph{tensor} objects that generate a Clifford algebra, thereby endowing the emergent field with fermionic statistics.
		%This section introduces the tensor-formatted hidden layer to output layer weighting parameters, and construct the fermionic quantum field theory explanation. 
        According to the complex-valued scalar field theory, the free propagator will connect the mutually conjugate fields, the main problem is to construct the Grassman number, or the anticommute characters of fermionic fields. Construct the anticommute matrices $\gamma_{h}$ via Pauli matrices. For $H=3$, set $\gamma_{h}^{(3)}\equiv\sigma_{h}$ ($h=1,2,3$). For $H$ being odd numbers and $H\geq5$, one can construct the gamma matrices by recursive definition as~\cite{DaviesSpinors2011,Park:2022pjv}
		\begin{eqnarray}
			&&\gamma_{h}^{(H)}\equiv-\sigma_{2}\otimes\gamma_{h}^{(H-2)},\ h=1,2,...,H-2,\\
			&&\gamma_{H-1}^{(H)}\equiv\sigma_{1}\otimes I_{\frac{d}{2}\times\frac{d}{2}},\\
			&&\gamma_{H}^{(H)}\equiv\sigma_{3}\otimes I_{\frac{d}{2}\times\frac{d}{2}},
		\end{eqnarray}
		with the matrices $\gamma_{h}^{(H)}$ meet the Clifford algebra $\{\gamma_{h}^{(H)},\gamma_{h'}^{(H)}\}=2\delta_{hh'}I_{d\times d}$ and $(\gamma_{h}^{(H)})^{\dag}=\gamma_{h}^{(H)}$, the dimensions of $\gamma_{h}^{(H)}$ should be $d=2^{(H-1)/2}$. Inspired by the section~\ref{QS_infinite_H}, only the last layer weight parameters control the spin character of the output field, % are connected to the bosonic and fermionic characters of the field $f$, 
        with the other parameters being the eigenvalues of the quantum field, transform these weighting parameters to the tensor-formatted coefficients by multiplying the gamma matrices as
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\varphi_{h}\rightarrow \varphi_{h}\gamma_{h},\ \varphi_{h}^{*}\rightarrow \varphi_{h}^{*} \gamma_{h}^{\dag},\ h=1,2,...,H.
		\end{eqnarray}
		Take the 4-pt correlation function as a example, there exist the trace calculation relation that
		\begin{eqnarray}\label{4-pt trace}
			&&\frac{1}{d}\left<\varphi_{h_{1}}^{*}\varphi_{h_{2}}^{*}\varphi_{h_{1}'}\varphi_{h_{2}'}\right>{\rm tr}[\gamma_{h_{1}}\gamma_{h_{2}}\gamma_{h_{1}'}\gamma_{h_{2}'}]\\
			&=&\frac{4\sigma_{\rm w}^{4}}{H^{2}}(2\delta_{h_{1}h_{2}}\delta_{h_{1}h_{1}'}\delta_{h_{1}h_{2}'}-\delta_{h_{1}h_{1}'}\delta_{h_{2}h_{2}'}+\delta_{h_{1}h_{2}'}\delta_{h_{2}h_{1}'}),\nonumber
		\end{eqnarray}
		with the last two terms inside the bracket give the anticommute characters of $\varphi_{h}$. 

		The generating functional can not be transformed to the fermionic condition using the traditional auxiliary field method, for the derivatives of the auxiliary field will not give the extra tensor-formatted field $f({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})$, considering the inequation that
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\frac{\delta}{\delta J({\bm y})}{\rm e}^{\int{\rm d}^{D}{\bm x}J({\bm x})f_{\gamma}({\bm Q},V|{\bm x})}\bigg|_{J=0}&\neq&f_{\gamma}({\bm Q},V|{\bm y}).
		\end{eqnarray}
		However, in the infinite width hidden layer condition, $H\rightarrow\infty$, the vertexes higher than 4-point vertex (including, as shown in appendix \ref{Example: Tensor weights 4-point correlation function}) vanish, and the Clifford algebra of $\gamma_{h}^{(H)}$ transforms to the Grassman algebra, the first term in Eq.~(\ref{4-pt trace}) being depressed, for example. Then the corresponding quantum field explanation of this tensor-formatted weighting parameters neural network becomes the fermionic quantum field.





		\section{summary}\label{summary}
        This paper presents a theoretical framework that rigorously connects complex-valued neural networks (CVNNs) to fermionic quantum field theory (QFT). By leveraging the generating functional analysis, we demonstrate that the statistical properties of the output function of CVNNs -- governed by the network's weight with generalization to tensor format -- mirror those of fermionic fields in QFT. The fermionic anticommutation relations arise naturally from the tensorization of the hidden-to-output weights where Clifford algebra matrices introduce non-commutativity. In the limit of infinite hidden-layer width, the networks's output converges to a Gaussian process suppressing higher-order interactions and reducing the effective action to a free fermionic field theory. 

        The reliance on independent Gaussian weight distributions and infinite-width approximations may oversimplify practical networks, where non-Gaussian fluctuations or finite-width effects could alter field-theoretic predictions. On the theoretical front, extending the framework to interacting fermionic systems (e.g., Yukawa or gauge theories) could deepen connections between deep learning and high-energy physics. Practically, the tensorization method provides a blueprint for encoding fermionic symmetries into neural architectures, potentially enhancing simulations of quantum many-body systems or lattice field theories. 

