Abstract
In this paper we give a criterion for the existence and stability of relaxation oscillations produced by slow-fast cycles in a class of predator-prey systems with a piecewise smooth functional response and a small predator's death rate. We also study upper bounds for the number of relaxation oscillations in such predator-prey systems. We extend the results of [Hsu, SIAM. Appl. Dyn. Sys., 2019] and [Ai and Yi, J. Dynam. Differential Equations, 2024] to the piecewise smooth case. In particular, we use a different approach to obtain the criterion, that is more simple than in the previous two papers. Our methods are based on geometric singular perturbation theory, entry-exit function, the construction of difference maps and the computation of divergence integrals. We apply our results to predator-prey systems with a Holling type I functional response and prove that they have exactly two relaxation oscillations.
