In this work, the authors investigate a new stochastic system of nonlinear partial differential equations modeling the Lotka-Volterra interactions of predator and prey in the presence of prey-taxis, spatial diffusion, and noise. First, they derive a macroscopic model from stochastic kinetic equations by using the micro-macro decomposition method. Second, they sketch the proof of the existence of weak martingale solutions by using a Faedo Galerkin method. Finally, they develop a one- and two-dimensional finite volume approximation for the stochastic kinetic and macroscopic models, respectively. The one-dimensional space numerical scheme is uniformly stable along the transition from kinetic to macroscopic regimes. They close with various numerical tests to illustrate the convergence of the numerical method and some features of the stochastic macro-scale system.

